/* Interactive lesson and question packs. Original practice questions; not official past questions. */
window.JAMB_CONTENT_PACKS = {"Use of English":{"lessons":[{"title":"1. Comprehension and Summary","note":"Learning objectives • Identify the main idea, supporting details, purpose, tone and attitude in a passage. • Distinguish facts from opinions and inference from information stated directly. • Summarise a passage without changing its meaning. Lesson Read the questions first when this helps you know what information to look for, then read the passage carefully. The main idea is the central point; supporting details explain or prove it. An inference is a conclusion supported by clues in the text, not a guess based on personal opinion. A writer's tone may be critical, humorous, persuasive, anxious or objective. Choose the tone that the words and context actually support. For summary questions, identify only the points requested. Combine related ideas, use your own concise wording, and avoid examples or repetition unless essential. Do not add opinions or facts that are absent from the passage. Subtopics • Main idea and topic sentences • Explicit details and evidence • Inference and implication • Writer's purpose, tone and attitude • Vocabulary in context • Summary points, concision and paraphrase Quick revision Ask: What is the passage mainly about? Which sentence supports my answer? Is my conclusion supported by the text? Have I added anything the writer did not say?","revision":["Identify the main idea, supporting details, purpose, tone and attitude in a passage.","Distinguish facts from opinions and inference from information stated directly.","Summarise a passage without changing its meaning.","Main idea and topic sentences","Explicit details and evidence","Inference and implication","Writer's purpose, tone and attitude","Vocabulary in context"]},{"title":"2. Lexis and Structure","note":"Learning objectives • Select words and grammatical structures that fit meaning and context. • Recognise collocations, register, word forms and common usage patterns. • Use surrounding words to infer an unfamiliar word's meaning. Lesson Lexis concerns vocabulary and word choice; structure concerns how words and clauses are arranged. A word may be grammatically possible but unsuitable for the context. For example, English normally says “make a decision”, not “do a decision”; this is a collocation. Check both meaning and grammar when choosing an answer. Word forms can change a sentence's function: decide (verb), decision (noun), decisive (adjective), decisively (adverb). Register matters too: formal writing may prefer “assist” where casual speech uses “help”. Use context rather than memorising isolated definitions. Subtopics • Word meaning in context • Collocations and idiomatic usage • Word formation and parts of speech • Formal, informal and technical register • Prefixes, suffixes and roots • Sentence completion Quick revision Read the complete sentence with each option inserted. Check meaning, grammar, collocation and register.","revision":["Select words and grammatical structures that fit meaning and context.","Recognise collocations, register, word forms and common usage patterns.","Use surrounding words to infer an unfamiliar word's meaning.","Word meaning in context","Collocations and idiomatic usage","Word formation and parts of speech","Formal, informal and technical register","Prefixes, suffixes and roots"]},{"title":"3. Antonyms and Synonyms","note":"Learning objectives • Choose a word with a similar meaning or an opposite meaning. • Use context to select the correct sense of a word. • Recognise that near-synonyms may differ in strength or usage. Lesson A synonym has a similar meaning; an antonym has an opposite meaning. Exact equivalence is uncommon: slim and thin are similar, but their tone can differ. A word can also have several senses. Bright can mean giving much light, intelligent, or vivid. The sentence determines which sense is relevant. For an antonym question, identify the meaning used in the sentence before selecting the opposite. Avoid choosing a word that is merely associated with the target word. Subtopics • Contextual synonyms • Contextual antonyms • Shades of meaning • Positive, neutral and negative connotations • Multiple meanings and polysemy Quick revision Define the target word as used in the question, then test each option against that definition.","revision":["Choose a word with a similar meaning or an opposite meaning.","Use context to select the correct sense of a word.","Recognise that near-synonyms may differ in strength or usage.","Contextual synonyms","Contextual antonyms","Shades of meaning","Positive, neutral and negative connotations","Multiple meanings and polysemy"]},{"title":"4. Sentence Interpretation","note":"Learning objectives • Interpret sentences that express implied meanings. • Recognise paraphrase, emphasis, contrast and logical relationships. • Avoid interpretations that exaggerate or reverse the original meaning. Lesson Sentence interpretation tests what a statement means in context, not just the literal meaning of each word. A phrase such as “She barely passed” means she passed, but only by a small margin. “Unless you hurry, you will miss the bus” means that failing to hurry will result in missing it. When selecting a paraphrase, preserve the original meaning, time, degree and logical relationship. Be careful with negatives, conditionals, comparisons and words such as only, hardly, unless and despite. Subtopics • Paraphrase and restatement • Implied meaning • Condition and consequence • Contrast and concession • Degree and emphasis • Negation and logical meaning Quick revision Check whether the option preserves who did what, when it happened, and the strength or condition of the statement.","revision":["Interpret sentences that express implied meanings.","Recognise paraphrase, emphasis, contrast and logical relationships.","Avoid interpretations that exaggerate or reverse the original meaning.","Paraphrase and restatement","Implied meaning","Condition and consequence","Contrast and concession","Degree and emphasis"]},{"title":"5. Idioms and Phrasal Verbs","note":"Learning objectives • Interpret common idioms as expressions with established meanings. • Understand phrasal verbs formed from a verb and a particle. • Use idioms and phrasal verbs appropriately in context. Lesson An idiom is an expression whose meaning cannot always be worked out literally. “Break the ice” means to make people feel more comfortable at the start of an interaction. A phrasal verb combines a verb with an adverb or preposition, often changing the verb's meaning: give up means stop trying; look after means take care of. Some phrasal verbs are separable (turn the light off / turn off the light), while others are not (look after the child, not normally look the child after). Pronouns usually go between the verb and particle in separable phrasal verbs: turn it off. Subtopics • Common idioms and their meanings • Phrasal verbs and particles • Separable and inseparable phrasal verbs • Literal versus figurative meaning • Context-appropriate usage Quick revision Learn each expression with a short example sentence rather than memorising a word-for-word translation.","revision":["Interpret common idioms as expressions with established meanings.","Understand phrasal verbs formed from a verb and a particle.","Use idioms and phrasal verbs appropriately in context.","Common idioms and their meanings","Phrasal verbs and particles","Separable and inseparable phrasal verbs","Literal versus figurative meaning","Context-appropriate usage"]},{"title":"6. Parts of Speech","note":"Learning objectives • Identify the grammatical function of words in sentences. • Distinguish nouns, pronouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, prepositions, conjunctions and interjections. • Recognise that one word can belong to different classes in different sentences. Lesson A noun names a person, place, thing or idea; a pronoun stands in for a noun phrase; a verb expresses an action, event or state. An adjective modifies a noun, while an adverb modifies a verb, adjective, adverb or sometimes a whole clause. A preposition shows a relationship, often of time, place or direction. A conjunction joins words, phrases or clauses. Class depends on use. In “I drink water” drink is a verb; in “Have a drink” it is a noun. Identify what the word does in the sentence, not only its spelling. Subtopics • Nouns and noun phrases • Pronouns and reference • Main, auxiliary and modal verbs • Adjectives and determiners • Adverbs • Prepositions and prepositional phrases • Coordinating and subordinating conjunctions • Interjections Quick revision Name the word class, then explain the word's function in the particular sentence.","revision":["Identify the grammatical function of words in sentences.","Distinguish nouns, pronouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs, prepositions, conjunctions and interjections.","Recognise that one word can belong to different classes in different sentences.","Nouns and noun phrases","Pronouns and reference","Main, auxiliary and modal verbs","Adjectives and determiners","Adverbs"]},{"title":"7. Concord and Agreement","note":"Learning objectives • Match subjects with appropriate verb forms. • Apply agreement rules to collective nouns, indefinite pronouns and coordinated subjects. • Avoid errors caused by words placed between the subject and verb. Lesson Concord means grammatical agreement. A singular subject normally takes a singular verb in the present tense: “The student writes.” A plural subject takes a plural verb: “The students write.” Do not let a nearby noun distract you: “The box of books is heavy” because the subject is box, not books. With neither…nor and either…or, the verb commonly agrees with the nearer subject in standard exam usage. Indefinite pronouns such as everyone, someone and each are grammatically singular: “Everyone is ready.” Collective nouns may take singular or plural agreement depending on whether the group is treated as one unit or as individuals; follow the sentence meaning and the convention expected in the question. Subtopics • Subject–verb agreement • Singular and plural subjects • Subjects separated from verbs • Indefinite pronouns • Either/or and neither/nor • Collective nouns • Agreement with quantities and measurements Quick revision Find the true subject first, determine whether it is singular or plural, then select the matching verb.","revision":["Match subjects with appropriate verb forms.","Apply agreement rules to collective nouns, indefinite pronouns and coordinated subjects.","Avoid errors caused by words placed between the subject and verb.","Subject–verb agreement","Singular and plural subjects","Subjects separated from verbs","Indefinite pronouns","Either/or and neither/nor"]},{"title":"8. Tenses and Sequence of Tenses","note":"Learning objectives • Use simple, continuous, perfect and perfect-continuous forms. • Match tense to time, duration, completion and relevance. • Maintain logical tense relationships in reported speech and complex sentences. Lesson Tense and aspect help show when an action occurs and how it unfolds. The present simple describes habits and general truths (“Water boils at 100°C at standard atmospheric pressure”). The present continuous describes an action in progress or a temporary situation. The present perfect connects a past event with the present; the past perfect marks an event earlier than another past event. Time expressions provide clues: since often marks a starting point; for often marks a duration. Do not choose tense by a single word alone—read the whole sentence and its time relationship. In reported speech, tense may shift when the reporting verb is in the past, but facts that remain generally true may retain the present tense. Subtopics • Present, past and future forms • Simple and continuous aspect • Perfect and perfect-continuous aspect • Time expressions: since, for, already, yet • Sequence of tenses • Conditional sentences • Reported speech Quick revision Mark the time of each action and decide whether it is habitual, ongoing, completed or earlier than another event.","revision":["Use simple, continuous, perfect and perfect-continuous forms.","Match tense to time, duration, completion and relevance.","Maintain logical tense relationships in reported speech and complex sentences.","Present, past and future forms","Simple and continuous aspect","Perfect and perfect-continuous aspect","Time expressions: since, for, already, yet","Sequence of tenses"]},{"title":"9. Question Tags","note":"Learning objectives • Form question tags using the correct auxiliary and pronoun. • Apply positive-statement/negative-tag and negative-statement/positive-tag patterns. • Recognise special forms such as I am, imperatives and let's. Lesson A positive statement usually takes a negative tag: “She is ready, isn't she?” A negative statement usually takes a positive tag: “They haven't left, have they?” Repeat the auxiliary or modal verb from the statement; if there is no auxiliary in the present or past simple, use do/does/did. Use a pronoun in the tag, not the full noun. Common special forms include “I'm late, aren't I?” and “Let's begin, shall we?” Intonation can make a tag a genuine question or a request for confirmation, but grammar questions usually focus on the correct form. Subtopics • Auxiliary and modal verbs in tags • Positive and negative polarity • Pronoun selection • Tags with simple tenses • Special forms: I am, let's, imperatives Quick revision Check statement polarity, identify the auxiliary, and replace the subject with the correct pronoun.","revision":["Form question tags using the correct auxiliary and pronoun.","Apply positive-statement/negative-tag and negative-statement/positive-tag patterns.","Recognise special forms such as I am, imperatives and let's.","Auxiliary and modal verbs in tags","Positive and negative polarity","Pronoun selection","Tags with simple tenses","Special forms: I am, let's, imperatives"]},{"title":"10. Punctuation and Spelling","note":"Learning objectives • Use full stops, commas, semicolons, colons, apostrophes and quotation marks correctly. • Distinguish possessives from contractions. • Recognise commonly confused spellings and punctuation that changes meaning. Lesson Punctuation helps readers understand sentence structure. Use a full stop to end a statement; a comma to separate items or mark certain introductory and non-essential elements; a semicolon to connect closely related independent clauses; and a colon to introduce an explanation or list. Do not join two complete sentences with only a comma. An apostrophe marks omission (don't) or possession (the student's book). Plural nouns do not automatically take apostrophes. Compare its (possessive) with it's (it is/it has), and their, there and they're. Spelling questions often test words that sound alike but have different meanings. Subtopics • Sentence-ending punctuation • Commas and lists • Semicolons and colons • Apostrophes and possession • Quotation marks • Capitalisation • Commonly confused words and spelling patterns Quick revision Read the sentence aloud, identify its clauses, and check whether each punctuation mark has a clear grammatical purpose.","revision":["Use full stops, commas, semicolons, colons, apostrophes and quotation marks correctly.","Distinguish possessives from contractions.","Recognise commonly confused spellings and punctuation that changes meaning.","Sentence-ending punctuation","Commas and lists","Semicolons and colons","Apostrophes and possession","Quotation marks"]},{"title":"11. Register and Word Choice","note":"Learning objectives • Select vocabulary suitable for a particular profession, setting or communicative purpose. • Recognise formal, informal, technical and specialised language. • Use context to identify the intended register. Lesson Register is the style of language suited to a situation, audience or field. A medical report may use diagnosis and symptoms; a casual conversation may use simpler wording. A formal letter usually avoids slang and overly casual contractions. Register questions often describe a setting—such as a court, hospital, classroom, market or newsroom—and ask for the word most appropriate to it. The best answer is not always the longest or most technical word. Choose the word that fits both the meaning and the situation. Subtopics • Formal and informal language • Professional and technical vocabulary • Language of public institutions • Audience and purpose • Appropriate and inappropriate word choice Quick revision Identify who is speaking, to whom, for what purpose and in what setting before choosing the expression.","revision":["Select vocabulary suitable for a particular profession, setting or communicative purpose.","Recognise formal, informal, technical and specialised language.","Use context to identify the intended register.","Formal and informal language","Professional and technical vocabulary","Language of public institutions","Audience and purpose","Appropriate and inappropriate word choice"]},{"title":"12. Oral English: Vowels","note":"Learning objectives • Distinguish vowel sounds from vowel letters. • Recognise common monophthongs and diphthongs in phonetic notation. • Compare vowel sounds by tongue position, openness and lip shape. Lesson A vowel letter is a written symbol; a vowel sound is what is pronounced. English spelling does not reliably predict pronunciation, so phonetic symbols are useful. A monophthong is a vowel sound produced with relatively stable tongue position; a diphthong involves movement from one vowel quality toward another within one syllable. Vowels can differ in tongue height, frontness/backness, lip rounding and length. When answering sound questions, compare pronunciation rather than spelling. Accents vary, so follow the pronunciation model and phonetic symbols specified by the syllabus or question. Subtopics • Vowel letters and vowel phonemes • Short and long vowel contrasts • Monophthongs • Diphthongs • IPA symbols and minimal pairs • Stress and vowel reduction Quick revision Say the words aloud, isolate the target vowel sound, and compare it with the options.","revision":["Distinguish vowel sounds from vowel letters.","Recognise common monophthongs and diphthongs in phonetic notation.","Compare vowel sounds by tongue position, openness and lip shape.","Vowel letters and vowel phonemes","Short and long vowel contrasts","Monophthongs","Diphthongs","IPA symbols and minimal pairs"]},{"title":"13. Oral English: Consonants","note":"Learning objectives • Classify consonants by place and manner of articulation and voicing. • Distinguish consonant phonemes that may be represented by similar spellings. • Recognise minimal pairs and common pronunciation contrasts. Lesson Consonants are described by place of articulation (where airflow is narrowed or stopped), manner of articulation (how airflow is affected) and voicing (whether the vocal folds vibrate). For example, /p/ and /b/ are both bilabial plosives, but /p/ is voiceless and /b/ is voiced. Letters and sounds do not always correspond one-to-one. The letter combination sh represents one sound in ship, while the letter x commonly represents two sounds /ks/ in box. Use phonetic symbols and pronunciation, not spelling alone. Subtopics • Place of articulation • Manner of articulation • Voiced and voiceless consonants • Plosives, fricatives, affricates, nasals and approximants • Minimal pairs • Consonant clusters and silent letters Quick revision For a pair of sounds, compare voicing, place and manner; identify exactly which feature changes.","revision":["Classify consonants by place and manner of articulation and voicing.","Distinguish consonant phonemes that may be represented by similar spellings.","Recognise minimal pairs and common pronunciation contrasts.","Place of articulation","Manner of articulation","Voiced and voiceless consonants","Plosives, fricatives, affricates, nasals and approximants","Minimal pairs"]},{"title":"14. Stress and Intonation","note":"Learning objectives • Identify syllable stress in words. • Recognise how stress can distinguish word classes or meanings. • Interpret common intonation patterns in questions, statements and emphasis. Lesson Word stress is the prominence given to one syllable in a word. Stress can change meaning or grammatical class: REcord is commonly a noun, while reCORD is commonly a verb. Sentence stress highlights important information. Intonation is the pattern of pitch movement across an utterance; it can signal completion, a question, uncertainty, contrast or emotion. Do not assume every question ends with rising intonation. Yes/no questions often rise, while many wh-questions fall, though context and speaker intention affect the pattern. In exam questions, pay attention to the specific word or syllable marked. Subtopics • Syllables and word stress • Stress shift in related words • Sentence stress and emphasis • Falling and rising intonation • Contrastive stress • Intonation in questions and statements Quick revision Mark the stressed syllable, then consider whether the pitch pattern changes the speaker's intention.","revision":["Identify syllable stress in words.","Recognise how stress can distinguish word classes or meanings.","Interpret common intonation patterns in questions, statements and emphasis.","Syllables and word stress","Stress shift in related words","Sentence stress and emphasis","Falling and rising intonation","Contrastive stress"]},{"title":"15. Cloze Passages","note":"Learning objectives • Use grammar, vocabulary, collocation and overall meaning to complete gaps. • Track references and logical links across sentences. • Select the option that fits the passage as a whole. Lesson A cloze passage removes words from a connected text. Each gap should be solved using both local grammar and the passage's overall meaning. First read the entire passage to understand the subject and tone. Then check the words immediately before and after the gap, the required part of speech, tense, agreement, collocation and logical connectors. After selecting an option, read the full sentence and passage again. A word can fit grammatically but break the meaning or connection between sentences. Subtopics • Grammar-based gaps • Vocabulary and collocation • Connectors and transition words • Pronoun reference • Tense and agreement • Whole-passage coherence Quick revision Use three checks for each gap: grammar, meaning and connection to the surrounding text.","revision":["Use grammar, vocabulary, collocation and overall meaning to complete gaps.","Track references and logical links across sentences.","Select the option that fits the passage as a whole.","Grammar-based gaps","Vocabulary and collocation","Connectors and transition words","Pronoun reference","Tense and agreement"]}],"questions":[{"q":"A writer's main idea is best described as","o":["the longest sentence in the passage","the central point the passage develops","an unfamiliar word in the passage","the reader's personal opinion"],"a":1,"explanation":"The main idea is the central point supported by the passage."},{"q":"Which is the best practice when writing a summary?","o":["Add personal opinions to make it persuasive","Copy every example and repeated detail","State the essential points concisely in your own words","Introduce information from another source"],"a":2,"explanation":"A summary preserves essential meaning while removing repetition and unnecessary details."},{"q":"Choose the word that correctly completes the sentence: “The committee will ___ a decision tomorrow.”","o":["do","make","create up","perform"],"a":1,"explanation":"“Make a decision” is the standard collocation."},{"q":"Which word correctly completes the sentence? “Her careful ___ prevented the error.”","o":["analyse","analysis","analytical","analytically"],"a":1,"explanation":"A noun is required as the subject of “prevented”."},{"q":"Choose the word nearest in meaning to reluctant.","o":["unwilling","eager","careless","certain"],"a":0,"explanation":"“Reluctant” means unwilling or hesitant."},{"q":"Choose the word opposite in meaning to scarce.","o":["rare","limited","abundant","small"],"a":2,"explanation":"“Abundant” means plentiful, the opposite of scarce."},{"q":"“She barely passed the examination” means that she","o":["did not take the examination","passed by a small margin","achieved the highest score","refused to receive her result"],"a":1,"explanation":"“Barely” indicates that the result was only just sufficient."},{"q":"“Unless Musa apologises, I will not speak to him” means that I will speak to Musa","o":["even if he never apologises","only if he apologises","before he has done anything","because he has already apologised"],"a":1,"explanation":"“Unless” introduces a condition; an apology is required before the speaker will speak to him."},{"q":"If a speaker says “Let us break the ice,” the speaker wants to","o":["damage something frozen","make people feel more comfortable","end the meeting immediately","start an argument"],"a":1,"explanation":"“Break the ice” means ease tension or begin friendly interaction."},{"q":"In the sentence “The nurse looked after the patient,” looked after means","o":["searched for","stared at","took care of","waited behind"],"a":2,"explanation":"“Look after” is a phrasal verb meaning take care of."},{"q":"What is the grammatical class of quickly in “The runner moved quickly”?","o":["Noun","Adjective","Adverb","Preposition"],"a":2,"explanation":"“Quickly” modifies the verb “moved”."},{"q":"In which sentence is light used as a verb?","o":["The light is bright.","Please light the candle.","This bag is light.","We saw a traffic light."],"a":1,"explanation":"“Light” describes the action of setting the candle burning."},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence.","o":["The basket of oranges are heavy.","The basket of oranges is heavy.","The basket of oranges were heavy today.","The basket of oranges have heavy."],"a":1,"explanation":"The subject is the singular noun “basket”; “of oranges” does not change the subject's number."},{"q":"Everyone in the classrooms ___ ready.","o":["are","were being","is","have"],"a":2,"explanation":"“Everyone” is grammatically singular and takes “is” in this sentence."},{"q":"By the time we arrived, the film ___.","o":["begins","has begun tomorrow","had begun","will begin"],"a":2,"explanation":"The past perfect marks an event completed before another past event."},{"q":"She has lived in Port Harcourt ___ 2022.","o":["for","since","during","by"],"a":1,"explanation":"“Since” introduces the starting point; “for” introduces a duration."},{"q":"“They have finished the work, ___?”","o":["haven't they","have they","don't they","didn't they"],"a":0,"explanation":"A positive statement takes a negative tag using the auxiliary “have”."},{"q":"“Let's begin the lesson, ___?”","o":["will we","shall we","do we","aren't we"],"a":1,"explanation":"The conventional tag after “Let's” is “shall we?”"},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","o":["After the rain stopped we continued, our journey.","After the rain stopped, we continued our journey.","After, the rain stopped we continued our journey.","After the rain stopped we, continued our journey."],"a":1,"explanation":"The introductory clause is followed by a comma, and no comma separates the verb from its object."},{"q":"Which sentence uses the apostrophe correctly?","o":["The teachers' room belongs to several teachers.","The teacher's are in class.","The teachers room's are open.","The teachers's room is open."],"a":0,"explanation":"The plural possessive “teachers'” shows that the room belongs to several teachers."},{"q":"Which word is most appropriate in a formal medical report?","o":["tummy ache","feeling rough","abdominal pain","a bad belly"],"a":2,"explanation":"“Abdominal pain” is a standard formal medical expression."},{"q":"Which expression is most suitable for a formal application letter?","o":["“I wanna join your school.”","“I wish to apply for admission.”","“Put me in, please.”","“Your place looks cool.”"],"a":1,"explanation":"It is clear, formal and appropriate to the purpose."},{"q":"A diphthong is a vowel sound that","o":["has no sound at all","moves from one vowel quality toward another within one syllable","is always written with two separate syllables","can occur only at the beginning of a word"],"a":1,"explanation":"A diphthong involves a change in vowel quality during one syllable."},{"q":"Why should pronunciation questions not be answered by spelling alone?","o":["Every letter always represents two sounds","English spelling always follows one fixed sound rule","The same letter or letter group can represent different sounds","Vowels never occur in spoken English"],"a":2,"explanation":"English spelling-to-sound relationships are not always one-to-one."},{"q":"The sounds /p/ and /b/ are both normally classified as","o":["bilabial plosives","dental fricatives","velar nasals","palatal approximants"],"a":0,"explanation":"Both are produced with the two lips and a complete closure; /p/ is voiceless and /b/ voiced."},{"q":"Which feature distinguishes /s/ from /z/ in standard English pronunciation?","o":["Place of articulation only","Voicing","Number of syllables","Word meaning in every context"],"a":1,"explanation":"/s/ is voiceless while /z/ is voiced; both are alveolar fricatives."},{"q":"In standard pronunciation, which pair commonly illustrates a stress difference between a noun and a verb?","o":["REcord (noun) / reCORD (verb)","table / chair","green / blue","fast / quickly"],"a":0,"explanation":"The noun commonly has first-syllable stress, while the verb commonly has second-syllable stress."},{"q":"Sentence stress is mainly used to","o":["remove all vowel sounds","highlight important or contrasting information","change every noun into a verb","make every sentence a question"],"a":1,"explanation":"Stress can signal focus, contrast and emphasis."},{"q":"Choose the best connector: “The road was flooded; ___, the team arrived on time.”","o":["therefore","nevertheless","because","for example"],"a":1,"explanation":"“Nevertheless” signals contrast between the flooded road and arriving on time."},{"q":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: “Although Tunde was tired, he ___ working until the task was complete.”","o":["persisted in","prevented","depended","objected"],"a":0,"explanation":"“Persisted in working” means continued despite difficulty."}]},"Mathematics":{"lessons":[{"title":"1. Number Bases","note":"A base tells how many digits are used before carrying to the next place. Decimal is base 10; binary is base 2. In base (b), place values are powers of (b). For example, (231_4=2(4^2)+3(4)+1=45_{10}). To convert a positive decimal integer to another base, divide repeatedly by the target base and read the remainders upwards. Addition and multiplication in a base follow the same place-value principle, but carrying occurs when a column reaches the base. Subtopics: place value; conversion between bases; addition, subtraction and multiplication; applications to number representation. Check: Convert (1011_2) to decimal: (1(8)+0(4)+1(2)+1=11).","revision":[]},{"title":"2. Fractions, Decimals and Percentages","note":"A fraction represents a ratio of integers with a non-zero denominator. To add or subtract fractions, use a common denominator; multiply numerators and denominators directly; divide by multiplying by the reciprocal. A percentage is a fraction out of 100: (p\\%=p/100). Percentage change is ((\\text{change}/\\text{original})\\times100\\%). Repeated percentage changes are applied successively, not simply added. Subtopics: equivalent fractions; four operations; recurring decimals; conversion among fractions, decimals and percentages; percentage increase/decrease; profit, loss, discount and simple applications. Worked example: 15% of 240 is (0.15\\times240=36).","revision":[]},{"title":"3. Indices and Logarithms","note":"For non-zero (a), (a^m a^n=a^{m+n}), (a^m/a^n=a^{m-n}), and ((a^m)^n=a^{mn}). Also (a^0=1), (a^{-n}=1/a^n), and (a^{1/n}=\\sqrt[n]{a}) where defined. A logarithm reverses exponentiation: (log_a x=y) means (a^y=x), with (a>0), (a\\ne1), and (x>0). The laws include (log_a(xy)=log_a x+log_a y) and (log_a(x/y)=log_a x-log_a y). Subtopics: laws of indices; negative and fractional powers; standard form; definition and laws of logarithms; solving exponential/logarithmic equations. Worked example: (2^x=32=2^5), so (x=5).","revision":[]},{"title":"4. Sets and Venn Diagrams","note":"A set is a well-defined collection. (A\\cup B) contains elements in either set; (A\\cap B) contains elements common to both; (A'\\) contains elements in the universal set but not in (A). For two finite sets, (n(A\\cup B)=n(A)+n(B)-n(A\\cap B)). The intersection is subtracted once because it was counted twice. Subtopics: notation and membership; subsets; universal and empty sets; union and intersection; complements; Venn diagrams; counting problems. Worked example: If 18 study Biology, 15 study Chemistry, and 6 study both, then (n(B\\cup C)=18+15-6=27).","revision":[]},{"title":"5. Ratio, Proportion and Rates","note":"A ratio compares quantities in the same units. Simplify a ratio by dividing each term by a common factor. In direct proportion, (y=kx); in inverse proportion, (y=k/x), where (k) is constant. Rates compare different quantities, such as distance per time or cost per item. Convert units before comparing or calculating. Subtopics: simplifying ratios; sharing in a ratio; direct and inverse proportion; rates; speed, distance and time; scale and unit conversion. Worked example: Share ₦1,200 in ratio 2:3. There are 5 parts; each part is ₦240, so the shares are ₦480 and ₦720.","revision":[]},{"title":"6. Approximation and Bounds","note":"Rounding replaces a number with a nearby value at a stated accuracy. Significant figures begin at the first non-zero digit; decimal places count digits after the decimal point. If a value rounds to 3.4 to the nearest 0.1, its lower bound is 3.35 and its upper bound is 3.45, using the usual lower-inclusive, upper-exclusive convention. For measurements given to a stated precision, bounds describe possible true values. Subtopics: decimal places; significant figures; rounding; estimation; upper and lower bounds; error and percentage error. Worked example: 7.846 to 2 decimal places is 7.85.","revision":[]},{"title":"7. Algebraic Expressions","note":"An algebraic expression combines variables, constants and operations. Like terms have the same variable parts and powers: (3x+5x=8x), but (3x+5x^2) cannot be combined. Expand brackets using the distributive law: (a(b+c)=ab+ac). Factorisation reverses expansion and may use a common factor, difference of squares, or quadratic factorisation. Subtopics: terms and coefficients; collecting like terms; expansion; common-factor factorisation; difference of two squares; algebraic fractions and substitution. Worked example: (3(x+4)-2x=3x+12-2x=x+12).","revision":[]},{"title":"8. Linear Equations and Inequalities","note":"An equation remains balanced when the same operation is applied to both sides. Solve by simplifying, collecting variable terms on one side and constants on the other, then dividing by the coefficient. For inequalities, multiplying or dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality sign. Simultaneous linear equations can be solved by substitution or elimination. Subtopics: one-variable equations; brackets and fractions; inequalities and number lines; simultaneous equations; word problems. Worked example: (3x-7=11\\Rightarrow3x=18\\Rightarrow x=6).","revision":[]},{"title":"9. Quadratic Equations","note":"A quadratic has form (ax^2+bx+c=0), (a\\ne0). Solve by factorisation, completing the square, or the quadratic formula (x=(-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac})/(2a)). The discriminant (D=b^2-4ac) tells the nature of roots: (D>0) gives two distinct real roots, (D=0) gives equal real roots, and (D<0) gives no real roots. Subtopics: factorisation; completing the square; quadratic formula; discriminant; forming equations; quadratic word problems. Worked example: (x^2-5x+6=0\\Rightarrow(x-2)(x-3)=0), so (x=2) or (3).","revision":[]},{"title":"10. Sequences and Series","note":"A sequence is an ordered list; a series is the sum of its terms. In an arithmetic progression (AP), the common difference is (d), and (T_n=a+(n-1)d). Its first (n) terms sum to (S_n=n(a+l)/2), where (l) is the last term. In a geometric progression (GP), the common ratio is (r), and (T_n=ar^{n-1}). For (r\\ne1), (S_n=a(r^n-1)/(r-1)). Subtopics: patterns; nth term of AP and GP; finite sums; arithmetic means; geometric means; applications. Worked example: AP 4, 7, 10,… has (a=4,d=3); its 10th term is (4+9(3)=31).","revision":[]},{"title":"11. Variation","note":"Variation describes how one quantity depends on another. Direct variation (y\\propto x) means (y=kx); inverse variation (y\\propto1/x) means (y=k/x). Joint variation may be (y=kxz), while combined variation may involve both direct and inverse factors. Find the constant (k) from given values before using the relationship to calculate unknowns. Subtopics: direct, inverse, joint and combined variation; powers in variation; determining constants; applied problems. Worked example: If (y) varies directly as (x), and (y=12) when (x=3), then (k=4); when (x=5), (y=20).","revision":[]},{"title":"12. Functions and Graphs","note":"A function assigns exactly one output to each permitted input. (f(x)) means the value of the function at input (x). The domain is the set of allowed inputs; the range is the set of outputs. For a straight line (y=mx+c), (m) is the gradient and (c) is the vertical intercept. Graphs help solve equations, identify intersections and interpret trends. Subtopics: function notation; domain and range; linear and quadratic graphs; gradient and intercepts; plotting tables; solving equations graphically; interpretation. Worked example: If (f(x)=2x+1), then (f(3)=7).","revision":[]},{"title":"13. Coordinate Geometry","note":"Points are represented by coordinates ((x,y)). The gradient between ((x_1,y_1)) and ((x_2,y_2)) is (m=(y_2-y_1)/(x_2-x_1)), where (x_2\\ne x_1). The midpoint is (((x_1+x_2)/2,(y_1+y_2)/2)), and the distance is (\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2}). A line can be written (y=mx+c). Subtopics: Cartesian coordinates; distance and midpoint; gradient; equations of straight lines; parallel and perpendicular lines; intersections. Worked example: The gradient from (1,2) to (3,8) is ((8-2)/(3-1)=3).","revision":[]},{"title":"14. Plane Geometry and Mensuration","note":"Geometry studies shapes and their properties. Triangle angles sum to 180°; angles on a straight line sum to 180°; angles around a point sum to 360°. Mensuration applies formulas for perimeter, area, surface area and volume. Keep units consistent and square or cube them for area or volume. Subtopics: angle properties; polygons; congruence and similarity; perimeter; area of triangles, quadrilaterals and circles; surface area and volume of solids; scale drawings. Key formulas: rectangle area (=lw); triangle area (=\\frac12 bh); circle area (=\\pi r^2); cylinder volume (=\\pi r^2h).","revision":[]},{"title":"15. Circle Geometry","note":"A circle is the set of points at a fixed distance from its centre. A radius joins centre to circumference; a diameter is twice the radius. Important results include: angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference standing on the same arc; angles in the same segment are equal; the angle in a semicircle is 90°; opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°; a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact. Subtopics: chords and arcs; angles in a circle; cyclic quadrilaterals; tangents; intersecting chords and secants; circle theorems. Study tip: Draw and label the circle before applying a theorem; identify which arc or chord the angles stand on.","revision":[]},{"title":"16. Trigonometry","note":"For a right triangle and acute angle \\theta, \\sin\\theta=\\text{opposite}/\\text{hypotenuse}, \\cos\\theta=\\text{adjacent}/\\text{hypotenuse}, and \\tan\\theta=\\text{opposite}/\\text{adjacent}. The identity \\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta=1 is useful for checking results. Sine rule and cosine rule extend calculations to non-right triangles. Subtopics: trigonometric ratios; special angles; bearings and elevation/depression; sine rule; cosine rule; area of a triangle; trigonometric graphs and identities. Worked example: In a right triangle, if opposite = 3 and hypotenuse = 5, then \\sin\\theta=3/5.","revision":[]},{"title":"17. Vectors","note":"A vector has magnitude and direction; a scalar has magnitude only. In two dimensions, vectors can be represented as columns or components. Add vectors by adding corresponding components; multiply by a scalar by multiplying each component. Magnitude of (a,b) is \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}. Displacement and velocity are vector quantities. Subtopics: scalar and vector quantities; representation; addition and subtraction; scalar multiplication; magnitude; position vectors; vector geometry. Worked example: (2,3)+(4,-1)=(6,2).","revision":[]},{"title":"18. Matrices and Determinants","note":"A matrix is a rectangular arrangement of numbers. Its order is rows × columns. Matrices of the same order can be added or subtracted entry by entry; matrix multiplication requires the number of columns of the first matrix to equal the number of rows of the second. For a 2×2 matrix \\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}, determinant is ad-bc. If the determinant is non-zero, its inverse exists. Subtopics: order and types; equality; addition and subtraction; multiplication; determinants; inverse of 2×2 matrices; solving simultaneous equations by matrices. Worked example: determinant of [[2,1],[3,4]] is (2(4)-1(3)=5).","revision":[]},{"title":"19. Permutation and Combination","note":"A permutation counts arrangements where order matters; a combination counts selections where order does not matter. (n!=n(n-1)\\cdots1), with (0!=1). The number of arrangements of (r) objects selected from (n) distinct objects is ^{n}P_r=n!/(n-r)!; the number of selections is ^{n}C_r=n!/[r!(n-r)!]. Subtopics: factorial notation; arrangements; selections; repeated restrictions; counting principles; practical word problems. Worked example: Selecting 2 students from 5 is ^{5}C_2=10.","revision":[]},{"title":"20. Probability","note":"Probability measures the chance of an event. For equally likely outcomes, (P(A)=n(A)/n(S)), where (S) is the sample space. Probability lies between 0 and 1. The complement rule is (P(A')=1-P(A)). For independent events, (P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)); for mutually exclusive events, (P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)). Do not assume events are independent unless stated or justified. Subtopics: sample spaces; events; equally likely outcomes; complements; mutually exclusive and independent events; combined events; tree diagrams. Worked example: A fair die has three even faces out of six, so (P(\\text{even})=3/6=1/2).","revision":[]},{"title":"21. Statistics and Data Representation","note":"Statistics involves collecting, organising, summarising and interpreting data. Mean is total divided by number of observations; median is the middle value after ordering; mode is the most frequent value. For grouped data, class midpoints and frequencies are used for estimates. Range is maximum minus minimum. Graphs should have labelled axes, appropriate scales and clear units. Subtopics: frequency tables; bar charts and histograms; pie charts; mean, median and mode; range; cumulative frequency; quartiles and interpretation. Worked example: For 2, 4, 4, 8, the mean is 18/4=4.5, median is 4 and mode is 4.","revision":[]},{"title":"22. Differentiation","note":"Differentiation finds a function's instantaneous rate of change or gradient. The power rule is \\frac{d}{dx}(x^n)=nx^{n-1}. Constants differentiate to zero, and sums can be differentiated term by term. A stationary point occurs where the derivative is zero; its nature may be investigated using the graph or second derivative where appropriate. Subtopics: gradient as a limit/instantaneous rate; power rule; derivatives of polynomials; tangent and normal; stationary points; increasing/decreasing behaviour; simple rate-of-change problems. Worked example: If (y=3x^2+2x-4), then (dy/dx=6x+2).","revision":[]},{"title":"23. Integration","note":"Integration reverses differentiation for finding antiderivatives and can calculate areas. The power rule is \\int x^n dx=x^{n+1}/(n+1)+C for (n\\ne-1). The constant (C) is included for indefinite integrals. A definite integral evaluates an antiderivative at the upper limit minus its value at the lower limit. Interpret area carefully when a graph lies below the axis. Subtopics: indefinite integrals; power rule; constant of integration; definite integrals; area under simple curves; checking by differentiation. Worked example: \\int 4x^3 dx=x^4+C.","revision":[]},{"title":"Topic review routine","note":"1. Explain each definition without looking. 2. Rework the worked example on paper. 3. Solve the matching MCQs without viewing the answer key. 4. Mark errors by subtopic and revise those subtopics. 5. Check the current official JAMB syllabus to ensure no prescribed objective is omitted.","revision":[]}],"questions":[{"q":"Convert 1011_2 to base ten.","o":["9","10","11","12"],"a":2,"explanation":"(8+2+1=11)."},{"q":"Express 23_{10} in base 2.","o":["10111_2","11011_2","11101_2","10011_2 ## 2. Fractions, Decimals and Percentages"],"a":0,"explanation":"(23=16+4+2+1), so (10111_2)."},{"q":"What is 15% of 240?","o":["24","30","36","40"],"a":2,"explanation":"(0.15\\times240=36)."},{"q":"A bag costs ₦8,000 and is discounted by 10%. What is the sale price?","o":["₦7,000","₦7,200","₦7,800","₦8,800 ## 3. Indices and Logarithms"],"a":1,"explanation":"10% of ₦8,000 is ₦800; price = ₦7,200."},{"q":"Simplify 2^3\\times2^4.","o":["64","128","256","32"],"a":1,"explanation":"Add exponents: (2^{3+4}=128)."},{"q":"If 3^x=81, find x.","o":["2","3","4","5 ## 4. Sets and Venn Diagrams"],"a":2,"explanation":"(81=3^4)."},{"q":"If n(A)=18, n(B)=15 and n(A\\cap B)=6, find n(A\\cup B).","o":["21","27","33","39"],"a":1,"explanation":"(18+15-6=27)."},{"q":"Which symbol represents elements common to both sets A and B?","o":["A\\cup B","A'","A\\cap B","A\\setminus B ## 5. Ratio, Proportion and Rates"],"a":2,"explanation":"Intersection means common elements."},{"q":"Share ₦1,200 in the ratio 2:3. What is the larger share?","o":["₦400","₦480","₦600","₦720"],"a":3,"explanation":"Five parts; one part ₦240; larger share (3\\times240=₦720)."},{"q":"A car travels 150 km in 3 hours at a constant speed. Find its speed.","o":["30 km/h","45 km/h","50 km/h","60 km/h ## 6. Approximation and Bounds"],"a":2,"explanation":"Speed = distance/time = 150/3 = 50 km/h."},{"q":"Round 7.846 to 2 decimal places.","o":["7.84","7.85","7.86","7.90"],"a":1,"explanation":"The third decimal digit is 6, so 7.84 rounds up to 7.85."},{"q":"A length rounds to 5.2 cm to the nearest 0.1 cm. Using the usual lower-inclusive convention, its possible values lie in","o":["5.1\\le x<5.2","5.15\\le x<5.25","5.2\\le x<5.3","5.0\\le x<5.4 ## 7. Algebraic Expressions"],"a":1,"explanation":"Half of 0.1 is 0.05, giving 5.15 to just below 5.25."},{"q":"Simplify 3(x+4)-2x.","o":["x+4","x+12","5x+12","x+6"],"a":1,"explanation":"Expand: (3x+12-2x=x+12)."},{"q":"Factorise x^2-9.","o":["(x-9)(x+1)","(x-3)^2","(x-3)(x+3)","x(x-9) ## 8. Linear Equations and Inequalities"],"a":2,"explanation":"Difference of squares: (x^2-3^2=(x-3)(x+3))."},{"q":"Solve 3x-7=11.","o":["4","5","6","7"],"a":2,"explanation":"(3x=18), so (x=6)."},{"q":"Solve -2x>8.","o":["x>-4","x<-4","x>4","x<4 ## 9. Quadratic Equations"],"a":1,"explanation":"Divide by −2 and reverse the inequality: (x<-4)."},{"q":"Solve x^2-5x+6=0.","o":["1 and 6","2 and 3","−2 and −3","0 and 6"],"a":1,"explanation":"((x-2)(x-3)=0)."},{"q":"What is the discriminant of x^2+4x+4=0?","o":["−4","0","4","16 ## 10. Sequences and Series"],"a":1,"explanation":"(b^2-4ac=16-16=0)."},{"q":"Find the 10th term of 4, 7, 10, 13, …","o":["28","30","31","34"],"a":2,"explanation":"(4+9(3)=31)."},{"q":"Find the sum of the first 5 terms of 2, 4, 6, 8, …","o":["20","25","30","32 ## 11. Variation"],"a":2,"explanation":"(2+4+6+8+10=30)."},{"q":"(y) varies directly as (x). If (y=12) when (x=3), find (y) when (x=5).","o":["15","18","20","24"],"a":2,"explanation":"(k=12/3=4), so (y=4(5)=20)."},{"q":"If (y) varies inversely as (x) and (y=6) when (x=4), find (y) when (x=8).","o":["2","3","6","12 ## 12. Functions and Graphs"],"a":1,"explanation":"(xy=k=24), so (y=24/8=3)."},{"q":"If (f(x)=2x+1), find (f(3)).","o":["5","6","7","8"],"a":2,"explanation":"(2(3)+1=7)."},{"q":"The gradient of the line (y=4x-3) is","o":["−3","−4","3","4 ## 13. Coordinate Geometry"],"a":3,"explanation":"In (y=mx+c), gradient is (m=4)."},{"q":"Find the gradient joining (1, 2) and (3, 8).","o":["2","3","4","6"],"a":1,"explanation":"((8-2)/(3-1)=3)."},{"q":"Find the midpoint of (2, 4) and (6, 8).","o":["(3, 5)","(4, 6)","(8, 12)","(2, 2) ## 14. Plane Geometry and Mensuration"],"a":1,"explanation":"Average coordinates: (4, 6)."},{"q":"Find the area of a triangle with base 10 cm and perpendicular height 6 cm.","o":["16 cm²","30 cm²","60 cm²","120 cm²"],"a":1,"explanation":"Area (=\\frac12(10)(6)=30\\text{ cm}^2)."},{"q":"The sum of the interior angles of a triangle is","o":["90°","180°","270°","360° ## 15. Circle Geometry"],"a":1,"explanation":"Triangle angle sum is 180°."},{"q":"An angle at the centre of a circle is 100°. What is the angle at the circumference standing on the same arc?","o":["25°","50°","100°","200°"],"a":1,"explanation":"Circumference angle is half the central angle: 50°."},{"q":"A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius at the","o":["centre only","opposite end of the diameter","point of contact","any point outside the circle ## 16. Trigonometry"],"a":2,"explanation":"Tangent is perpendicular to radius at contact."},{"q":"In a right triangle, the opposite side is 3 and the hypotenuse is 5. Find \\sin\\theta.","o":["3/5","4/5","5/3","3/4"],"a":0,"explanation":"Sine = opposite/hypotenuse = 3/5."},{"q":"If \\tan\\theta=1 and \\theta is acute, then \\theta=","o":["30°","45°","60°","90° ## 17. Vectors"],"a":1,"explanation":"( an45°=1)."},{"q":"Find (2,3)+(4,-1).","o":["(6, 2)","(2, 4)","(6, 4)","(−2, 2)"],"a":0,"explanation":"Add components: (2+4, 3−1)=(6,2)."},{"q":"Find the magnitude of vector (3, 4).","o":["4","5","7","25 ## 18. Matrices and Determinants"],"a":1,"explanation":"Magnitude (=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5)."},{"q":"Find the determinant of \\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix}.","o":["5","8","11","14"],"a":0,"explanation":"(2(4)-1(3)=5)."},{"q":"A matrix with 2 rows and 3 columns has order","o":["3 × 2","2 × 3","5 × 1","2 × 2 ## 19. Permutation and Combination"],"a":1,"explanation":"Matrix order is rows × columns."},{"q":"How many ways can 2 students be selected from 5 students?","o":["5","10","20","25"],"a":1,"explanation":"(^{5}C_2=5!/(2!3!)=10)."},{"q":"Evaluate 4!.","o":["12","16","24","32 ## 20. Probability"],"a":2,"explanation":"(4!=4\\times3\\times2\\times1=24)."},{"q":"A fair die is thrown. What is the probability of obtaining an even number?","o":["1/6","1/3","1/2","2/3"],"a":2,"explanation":"Three even outcomes among six: (3/6=1/2)."},{"q":"If (P(A)=0.35), find (P(A')).","o":["0.35","0.55","0.65","1.35 ## 21. Statistics and Data Representation"],"a":2,"explanation":"Complement = (1-0.35=0.65)."},{"q":"Find the mean of 2, 4, 4 and 8.","o":["4","4.5","5","6"],"a":1,"explanation":"Mean = 18/4 = 4.5."},{"q":"Find the median of 3, 9, 2, 7, 5.","o":["3","5","7","9 ## 22. Differentiation"],"a":1,"explanation":"Ordered data: 2,3,5,7,9; middle value is 5."},{"q":"Differentiate (y=3x^2+2x-4) with respect to (x).","o":["(3x+2)","(6x+2)","(6x-4)","(3x^2+2)"],"a":1,"explanation":"Power rule gives (6x+2)."},{"q":"The derivative of a constant is","o":["1","the constant itself","0","undefined in every case ## 23. Integration"],"a":2,"explanation":"A constant has no rate of change."},{"q":"Evaluate \\int 4x^3\\,dx.","o":["(12x^2+C)","(x^4+C)","(4x^4+C)","(x^3+C)"],"a":1,"explanation":"Power rule gives (x^4+C)."},{"q":"Evaluate \\int_0^2 2x\\,dx.","o":["2","4","6","8 ---"],"a":1,"explanation":"Antiderivative is (x^2); (2^2-0^2=4)."}]},"Biology":{"lessons":[{"title":"1. Characteristics and Classification of Living Things","note":"Subtopics: nutrition, respiration, movement, growth, excretion, reproduction, sensitivity; classification; binomial nomenclature. Living things carry out life processes. Respiration releases usable energy from food; it is not the same as breathing. Excretion removes metabolic waste, while egestion removes undigested food. Classification groups organisms by shared characteristics. In binomial nomenclature, the genus begins with a capital letter and the species name with a lowercase letter; both are italicised when typed. Check yourself: Explain the difference between excretion and egestion; state the purpose of classification.","revision":[]},{"title":"2. The Cell","note":"Subtopics: cell theory, plant and animal cells, organelles, prokaryotes and eukaryotes, specialised cells. The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life. The nucleus contains genetic material; the cell membrane controls movement in and out; mitochondria release energy during aerobic respiration; ribosomes make proteins. Plant cells usually have a cellulose cell wall, chloroplasts in photosynthetic tissues and a large permanent vacuole. Bacteria are prokaryotic and lack a membrane-bound nucleus. Check yourself: Compare plant and animal cells; link an organelle to its function.","revision":[]},{"title":"3. Levels of Organisation","note":"Subtopics: cell, tissue, organ, system, organism; examples in plants and animals. Similar cells form tissues; tissues combine to form organs; organs work together in systems. Examples include muscle tissue, the heart and the circulatory system. In plants, xylem and phloem are transport tissues. Check yourself: Arrange the levels from simplest to most complex.","revision":[]},{"title":"4. Cell Division","note":"Subtopics: interphase, mitosis, meiosis, chromosome number, importance. Mitosis produces two genetically similar daughter cells and supports growth, repair and asexual reproduction. Meiosis produces haploid cells for sexual reproduction and introduces variation. DNA is replicated before division during interphase; interphase is not itself a stage of mitosis. Check yourself: Why must gametes be haploid?","revision":[]},{"title":"5. Nutrition in Plants","note":"Subtopics: photosynthesis, chlorophyll, light and dark-dependent reactions, limiting factors, mineral nutrition. Photosynthesis uses light energy to make carbohydrates from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen. Chlorophyll absorbs light. Light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature can limit the rate. Nitrates are needed to make amino acids and proteins; magnesium is needed for chlorophyll. Check yourself: Write the word equation for photosynthesis and name two limiting factors.","revision":[]},{"title":"6. Nutrition in Animals","note":"Subtopics: food classes, balanced diet, digestion, enzymes, absorption and assimilation. Carbohydrates supply energy, proteins support growth and repair, fats store energy and insulate, while vitamins, minerals, fibre and water have essential supporting roles. Amylase digests starch; proteases digest proteins; lipases digest fats. Bile emulsifies fats and helps neutralise acidic chyme but is not an enzyme. Most absorption occurs in the small intestine through villi. Check yourself: Match digestive enzymes to their substrates.","revision":[]},{"title":"7. Transport in Plants","note":"Subtopics: xylem, phloem, root hairs, transpiration, translocation. Xylem transports water and mineral salts mainly upward and also supports the plant. Phloem translocates sucrose and other organic solutes from sources to sinks. Root hairs increase absorptive surface area. Transpiration is water-vapour loss from aerial plant parts, chiefly through stomata; it helps pull water through the xylem. Check yourself: Distinguish transpiration from translocation.","revision":[]},{"title":"8. Transport in Animals","note":"Subtopics: blood components, heart, vessels, circulation, lymph. Plasma transports dissolved substances; red blood cells carry oxygen using haemoglobin; white blood cells defend against pathogens; platelets aid clotting. Arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins return blood to it, and capillaries permit exchange. Humans have double circulation: pulmonary and systemic. Check yourself: Compare arteries, veins and capillaries.","revision":[]},{"title":"9. Respiration","note":"Subtopics: aerobic and anaerobic respiration, gas exchange, respiratory organs. Aerobic respiration releases energy from glucose using oxygen and produces carbon dioxide and water. Anaerobic respiration releases less energy without oxygen; in human muscles it produces lactate, while yeast produces ethanol and carbon dioxide. Gas exchange surfaces are thin, moist and have a large surface area. Check yourself: Compare the products of anaerobic respiration in muscles and yeast.","revision":[]},{"title":"10. Excretion and Homeostasis","note":"Subtopics: kidney, nephron, liver, lungs, skin, water and temperature regulation. Excretion removes metabolic waste. Kidneys filter blood, selectively reabsorb useful substances and regulate water and salts. The liver deaminates excess amino acids and converts toxic ammonia to urea. Homeostasis keeps internal conditions within suitable limits; hormones and negative feedback often regulate it. Check yourself: Explain why urea is formed and how kidneys help regulate water balance.","revision":[]},{"title":"11. Support and Movement","note":"Subtopics: skeleton, joints, muscles, plant support, tropisms. The skeleton supports and protects the body and provides attachment for muscles. At a movable joint, muscles often work in antagonistic pairs: one contracts while the other relaxes. Plants gain support from turgor and strengthening tissues. Tropisms are directional growth responses to stimuli such as light or gravity. Check yourself: Explain how a biceps–triceps pair moves the forearm.","revision":[]},{"title":"12. Coordination and Control","note":"Subtopics: nervous system, reflex arc, sense organs, hormones, endocrine glands. Neurons transmit impulses. A reflex arc commonly involves a receptor, sensory neuron, relay neuron, motor neuron and effector. Hormones are chemical messengers carried in blood to target cells. The pancreas produces insulin, which lowers blood glucose, and glucagon, which raises it. Check yourself: Compare nervous and hormonal coordination.","revision":[]},{"title":"13. Reproduction in Plants","note":"Subtopics: flower parts, pollination, fertilisation, seed and fruit formation, germination, vegetative propagation. The anther produces pollen and the stigma receives it. Pollination is pollen transfer from anther to stigma; fertilisation is fusion of gametes. After fertilisation, the ovule develops into a seed and the ovary usually develops into a fruit. Germination requires suitable water, oxygen and temperature. Check yourself: Distinguish pollination from fertilisation.","revision":[]},{"title":"14. Reproduction in Animals and Humans","note":"Subtopics: male and female reproductive systems, menstrual cycle, fertilisation, pregnancy, birth control and reproductive health. Sperm are produced in the testes; ova mature in the ovaries. Fertilisation normally occurs in the oviduct. The placenta allows exchange between maternal and foetal blood without normally mixing the two blood supplies directly. Reproductive health includes accurate information, hygiene and prevention of sexually transmitted infections. Check yourself: State the functions of the placenta and uterus.","revision":[]},{"title":"15. Growth and Development","note":"Subtopics: growth measurement, development, metamorphosis, plant growth. Growth involves a lasting increase in size or dry mass, often through cell division and enlargement. Development includes changes in form and function. Incomplete metamorphosis has egg, nymph and adult stages; complete metamorphosis has egg, larva, pupa and adult stages. Check yourself: Compare complete and incomplete metamorphosis.","revision":[]},{"title":"16. Genetics and Inheritance","note":"Subtopics: genes, alleles, genotype, phenotype, dominant and recessive alleles, monohybrid crosses, sex determination. Genes are DNA sections that influence inherited traits. Genotype is an organism's allele combination; phenotype is its observable characteristics influenced by genes and environment. For a simple cross Aa × Aa, the expected genotype ratio is 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa, assuming complete dominance and equal segregation. Check yourself: Use a Punnett square to predict offspring genotypes.","revision":[]},{"title":"17. Variation and Evolution","note":"Subtopics: continuous and discontinuous variation, mutation, natural selection, adaptation, evidence for evolution. Continuous variation shows a range, such as height; discontinuous variation has distinct categories, such as some blood groups. Mutation creates new genetic variants. Natural selection occurs when heritable traits that improve reproductive success become more common over generations. Individuals do not evolve intentionally in response to need. Check yourself: Give examples of both types of variation and explain natural selection.","revision":[]},{"title":"18. Ecology and Ecosystems","note":"Subtopics: habitat, population, community, ecosystem, biotic and abiotic factors, food chains and webs. A habitat is where an organism lives; a population is members of one species in an area; a community includes populations of different species. Ecosystems include organisms and their physical environment. Producers capture energy, consumers feed on organisms and decomposers break down dead material. Energy is lost between trophic levels, so food chains are usually short. Check yourself: Distinguish habitat, population and community.","revision":[]},{"title":"19. Energy Flow and Nutrient Cycles","note":"Subtopics: food pyramids, energy transfer, carbon and nitrogen cycles, decomposers. Energy flows through ecosystems and is not recycled; matter is recycled. Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide from air, while respiration, decomposition and combustion return it. Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert atmospheric nitrogen into usable compounds; decomposers return nitrogen compounds to soil. Check yourself: Explain why energy decreases at successive trophic levels.","revision":[]},{"title":"20. Population Studies and Adaptation","note":"Subtopics: population size, density, competition, carrying capacity, predator–prey relationships, adaptations. Population size changes with births, deaths, immigration and emigration. Competition increases when resources are limited. Carrying capacity is the population an environment can sustain under given conditions. Structural, behavioural and physiological adaptations improve survival or reproduction in a particular environment. Check yourself: List four processes that change population size.","revision":[]},{"title":"21. Microorganisms and Disease","note":"Subtopics: bacteria, viruses, fungi, protozoa, pathogens, transmission, immunity and prevention. Microorganisms include useful and harmful forms. Viruses replicate only inside host cells. Pathogens cause disease and may spread through air, water, food, body fluids or vectors. Hygiene, vaccination, safe water, sanitation and appropriate treatment help control infectious disease. Antibiotics act against susceptible bacteria, not viruses. Check yourself: Explain why antibiotics do not cure viral infections.","revision":[]},{"title":"22. Conservation of Natural Resources","note":"Subtopics: biodiversity, pollution, deforestation, erosion, sustainable use, protected areas. Biodiversity includes genetic, species and ecosystem diversity. Habitat destruction, pollution, overexploitation and invasive species can reduce it. Conservation includes habitat protection, restoration, sustainable harvesting, pollution control and environmental education. Check yourself: Name human activities that threaten biodiversity and suggest remedies.","revision":[]},{"title":"23. Soil and Water","note":"Subtopics: soil profile, soil organisms, soil types, water cycle, soil fertility and erosion. Soil contains mineral particles, organic matter, air and water. Humus improves structure and nutrient availability. Soil texture affects drainage and water retention. The water cycle includes evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation and runoff. Vegetation reduces erosion by protecting soil and slowing surface flow. Check yourself: Explain two ways to maintain soil fertility.","revision":[]},{"title":"24. Practical Biology and Biological Drawing","note":"Subtopics: observation, magnification, drawing rules, tables, graphs, food tests and microscope use. Scientific drawings should be large, clear, proportional and labelled with straight ruled lines; avoid shading unless instructed. Magnification = image size ÷ actual size, using the same units. Record observations before conclusions. Common food tests include iodine for starch, Biuret for protein and emulsion test for lipids. Check yourself: Calculate magnification and state positive results for common food tests.","revision":[]},{"title":"25. Agriculture and Applied Biology","note":"Subtopics: crop and livestock improvement, pests, diseases, soil management, biotechnology and public health. Biology is applied in breeding, pest management, food production, sanitation and conservation. Crop rotation can maintain soil fertility and interrupt pest cycles. Biological control uses living organisms to reduce pests, but ecological impacts should be considered before release. Check yourself: Explain how crop rotation and biological control can support sustainable farming.","revision":[]},{"title":"Revision Checklist","note":"• Define key terms precisely and distinguish similar processes. • Practise labelled diagrams: cell, flower, heart, nephron, digestive system and reproductive systems. • Learn food tests, ecological relationships, genetics ratios and biological calculations. • Compare every topic against the current official JAMB syllabus before treating this pack as exhaustive.","revision":["Define key terms precisely and distinguish similar processes.","Practise labelled diagrams: cell, flower, heart, nephron, digestive system and reproductive systems.","Learn food tests, ecological relationships, genetics ratios and biological calculations.","Compare every topic against the current official JAMB syllabus before treating this pack as exhaustive."]}],"questions":[{"q":"Which process removes metabolic waste from an organism?","o":["Egestion","Excretion","Digestion","Assimilation"],"a":1,"explanation":"Excretion removes metabolic waste; egestion removes undigested food."},{"q":"In binomial nomenclature, which part of a scientific name begins with a capital letter?","o":["Species","Family","Genus","Order"],"a":2,"explanation":"The genus name starts with a capital letter."},{"q":"Which organelle is the main site of aerobic respiration?","o":["Ribosome","Mitochondrion","Vacuole","Golgi body"],"a":1,"explanation":"Mitochondria are major sites of aerobic respiration."},{"q":"Which structure is normally present in a plant cell but absent in an animal cell?","o":["Cell membrane","Cytoplasm","Cellulose cell wall","Ribosome"],"a":2,"explanation":"Plant cell walls contain cellulose."},{"q":"The correct order of biological organisation is","o":["organ–cell–tissue–system","cell–tissue–organ–system","tissue–cell–system–organ","cell–organ–tissue–system"],"a":1,"explanation":"Cells form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs form systems."},{"q":"Mitosis is important mainly for","o":["producing haploid gametes","growth and repair","reducing chromosome number","creating gametes by meiosis"],"a":1,"explanation":"Mitosis supports growth, repair and replacement."},{"q":"Which mineral is required for chlorophyll formation?","o":["Magnesium","Sodium","Iodine","Chlorine"],"a":0,"explanation":"Magnesium is part of the chlorophyll molecule."},{"q":"Which factor can limit photosynthesis when light is abundant?","o":["Carbon dioxide concentration","Sound intensity","Soil colour alone","Wind direction alone"],"a":0,"explanation":"Carbon dioxide can limit the rate even when light is sufficient."},{"q":"Which enzyme begins starch digestion in humans?","o":["Lipase","Amylase","Pepsin","Trypsin"],"a":1,"explanation":"Amylase breaks starch into smaller carbohydrates."},{"q":"Bile assists fat digestion by","o":["acting as a protein enzyme","emulsifying fat","converting glucose to starch","digesting cellulose"],"a":1,"explanation":"Bile emulsifies fat and helps provide suitable pH; it is not an enzyme."},{"q":"Which tissue transports water and mineral salts in plants?","o":["Phloem","Epidermis","Xylem","Cortex only"],"a":2,"explanation":"Xylem transports water and mineral salts."},{"q":"Loss of water vapour from plant aerial parts is called","o":["translocation","transpiration","transamination","germination"],"a":1,"explanation":"Transpiration is water-vapour loss from plant parts."},{"q":"Which blood component transports most oxygen?","o":["Platelets","Red blood cells","Plasma proteins only","White blood cells"],"a":1,"explanation":"Haemoglobin in red blood cells carries most oxygen."},{"q":"Capillaries are well suited for exchange because they","o":["have very thick walls","have thin walls","contain valves throughout","carry blood only away from the heart"],"a":1,"explanation":"Thin capillary walls shorten the diffusion distance."},{"q":"Aerobic respiration of glucose produces","o":["ethanol only","lactic acid only","carbon dioxide and water","starch and oxygen"],"a":2,"explanation":"Aerobic respiration forms carbon dioxide and water while releasing energy."},{"q":"In human muscles, anaerobic respiration produces","o":["ethanol","lactate","urea","oxygen"],"a":1,"explanation":"Human muscle anaerobic respiration forms lactate."},{"q":"Excess amino acids are deaminated mainly in the","o":["liver","bladder","pancreas","trachea"],"a":0,"explanation":"The liver deaminates excess amino acids and forms urea."},{"q":"The functional unit of the kidney is the","o":["alveolus","nephron","villus","neuron"],"a":1,"explanation":"The nephron is the kidney's functional unit."},{"q":"At a typical antagonistic elbow joint, when the biceps contracts the triceps","o":["contracts equally","relaxes","becomes a bone","stops receiving blood"],"a":1,"explanation":"Antagonistic muscles act in opposite ways."},{"q":"A reflex action is generally","o":["slow and always conscious","rapid and automatic","controlled only by hormones","unrelated to neurons"],"a":1,"explanation":"Reflexes are rapid, automatic responses."},{"q":"Which plant structure produces pollen?","o":["Stigma","Ovary","Anther","Sepal"],"a":2,"explanation":"Anthers produce pollen."},{"q":"Fertilisation in flowering plants is the","o":["transfer of pollen to stigma","fusion of male and female gametes","opening of a flower","dispersal of a seed"],"a":1,"explanation":"Fertilisation is gamete fusion; pollination is pollen transfer."},{"q":"In humans, fertilisation normally occurs in the","o":["oviduct","cervix","uterus wall","vagina"],"a":0,"explanation":"Fertilisation normally occurs in the oviduct."},{"q":"The placenta mainly enables","o":["direct mixing of all maternal and foetal blood","exchange of materials between maternal and foetal circulations","production of sperm","digestion of foetal food"],"a":1,"explanation":"The placenta enables exchange while normally keeping the blood supplies separate."},{"q":"The stage between larva and adult in complete insect metamorphosis is the","o":["nymph","pupa","zygote","embryo only"],"a":1,"explanation":"Complete metamorphosis includes a pupal stage."},{"q":"In a cross Aa × Aa with complete dominance, the expected genotype ratio is","o":["3:1","1:1","1:2:1","9:3:3:1"],"a":2,"explanation":"Aa × Aa gives 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa."},{"q":"The observable expression of a trait is its","o":["genotype","phenotype","allele","chromosome number"],"a":1,"explanation":"Phenotype is observable expression."},{"q":"Which is an example of continuous variation?","o":["Human height","ABO blood group","Presence or absence of a particular seed shape category","Tongue-rolling category as taught in a simple classroom example"],"a":0,"explanation":"Height varies across a continuous range."},{"q":"In natural selection, a heritable advantageous trait may become common because its carriers","o":["always choose to change their genes","leave more surviving offspring on average","stop competing","never experience mutation"],"a":1,"explanation":"Natural selection changes trait frequencies through differential reproductive success."},{"q":"A population consists of","o":["all species on Earth","organisms of one species in a given area","only non-living parts of a habitat","all ecosystems in a country"],"a":1,"explanation":"A population is one species in an area at a particular time."},{"q":"Organisms that make organic food from inorganic substances are","o":["producers","primary consumers","parasites only","scavengers only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Producers make organic food, usually using light energy."},{"q":"Why are food chains usually short?","o":["Energy is lost at each trophic transfer","Energy is recycled completely","Producers contain no energy","Consumers do not respire"],"a":0,"explanation":"Energy is lost as heat and through life processes at each transfer."},{"q":"Nitrogen fixation makes atmospheric nitrogen available by converting it into","o":["usable nitrogen compounds","oxygen gas","pure protein directly","carbon dioxide"],"a":0,"explanation":"Nitrogen fixation produces compounds organisms can use."},{"q":"Carrying capacity refers to","o":["the maximum population an environment can sustain under given conditions","number of species in a food chain","total rainfall in a year","the age of an organism"],"a":0,"explanation":"Carrying capacity depends on available resources and conditions."},{"q":"Which statement about viruses is correct?","o":["They reproduce independently in soil","They require host cells to replicate","They are all killed by antibiotics","They are larger than all bacteria"],"a":1,"explanation":"Viruses need host cells for replication."},{"q":"Antibiotics are designed primarily to act against","o":["susceptible bacteria","every virus","all genetic diseases","nutrient deficiencies"],"a":0,"explanation":"Antibiotics target bacteria, not viruses."},{"q":"Which action best supports biodiversity conservation?","o":["Destroying wetlands","Protecting and restoring habitats","Releasing any non-native species","Increasing illegal wildlife trade"],"a":1,"explanation":"Habitat protection and restoration help conserve species and ecosystems."},{"q":"Humus is important because it","o":["is only pure sand","contributes organic matter and improves soil fertility","prevents all plant growth","contains no nutrients"],"a":1,"explanation":"Humus adds organic matter and supports soil structure and fertility."},{"q":"A positive iodine test for starch usually changes the iodine solution to","o":["blue-black","bright red","colourless gas","milky white"],"a":0,"explanation":"Iodine turns blue-black when starch is present."},{"q":"Magnification is calculated as","o":["actual size ÷ image size","image size ÷ actual size","image size + actual size","actual size × image size"],"a":1,"explanation":"Use the same units for image and actual size."},{"q":"Which food test is commonly used for protein?","o":["Iodine test","Biuret test","Emulsion test","Limewater test"],"a":1,"explanation":"Biuret reagent tests for protein."},{"q":"Crop rotation can help farmers by","o":["guaranteeing no pests ever occur","improving soil management and disrupting some pest cycles","removing all soil organisms","preventing photosynthesis"],"a":1,"explanation":"Rotation can maintain soil fertility and interrupt some pest and disease cycles."},{"q":"Which statement correctly compares egestion and excretion?","o":["Both mean removing metabolic waste","Egestion removes undigested food; excretion removes metabolic waste","Excretion removes undigested food only","Neither removes material from the body"],"a":1,"explanation":"These terms describe different removal processes."},{"q":"Which hormone lowers blood glucose concentration?","o":["Insulin","Glucagon","Adrenaline only","Thyroxine only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Insulin promotes uptake and storage of glucose, lowering blood glucose."},{"q":"Which is an abiotic factor in an ecosystem?","o":["Fungi","Grass","Temperature","Earthworm"],"a":2,"explanation":"Temperature is non-living; the others are organisms."},{"q":"The main function of root hairs is to","o":["produce pollen","increase surface area for absorption","release seeds","make glucose by photosynthesis"],"a":1,"explanation":"Root hairs increase the absorptive surface area."},{"q":"Which cells are primarily responsible for antibody production after differentiation?","o":["B lymphocytes/plasma cells","Red blood cells","Platelets","Xylem cells"],"a":0,"explanation":"Plasma cells derived from B lymphocytes secrete antibodies."},{"q":"Which process returns carbon dioxide to the atmosphere?","o":["Photosynthesis only","Respiration","Mineral absorption","Pollination"],"a":1,"explanation":"Respiration releases carbon dioxide; photosynthesis takes it up."},{"q":"A large, moist surface with a good blood supply is an adaptation for","o":["efficient gas exchange","reducing all diffusion","preventing oxygen entry","storing urine"],"a":0,"explanation":"Large area, moisture and short diffusion distance aid gas exchange."},{"q":"Which is a sound practice for a biological drawing?","o":["Use heavy shading","Make it small and crowded","Use clear outlines and straight label lines","Draw labels crossing each other"],"a":2,"explanation":"Biological drawings should be clear, proportional and neatly labelled."}]},"Chemistry":{"lessons":[{"title":"1. Nature of Matter and Separation Techniques","note":"Subtopics: elements, compounds, mixtures, physical and chemical changes, filtration, crystallisation, distillation, chromatography, sublimation. An element contains one type of atom; a compound contains elements chemically combined in fixed proportions; a mixture contains substances physically combined. Filtration separates an insoluble solid from a liquid. Simple distillation recovers a solvent from a solution; fractional distillation separates miscible liquids with different boiling points. Chromatography separates components by their different movement between stationary and mobile phases. Check yourself: Choose a separation method for sand and water, salt solution, and ink.","revision":[]},{"title":"2. Particulate Nature and Atomic Structure","note":"Subtopics: atoms, molecules, protons, neutrons, electrons, atomic number, mass number, isotopes. Protons carry +1 charge, electrons −1, and neutrons have no charge. Atomic number equals proton number; mass number equals protons plus neutrons. In a neutral atom, protons equal electrons. Isotopes are atoms of the same element with equal proton numbers but different neutron numbers. Check yourself: Find the numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons in a neutral atom from its notation.","revision":[]},{"title":"3. Periodic Table and Periodicity","note":"Subtopics: groups, periods, metals and non-metals, atomic radius, ionisation energy, electronegativity, valency. Elements in a group have related outer-shell electron arrangements and often similar chemical properties. Across a period, nuclear charge generally increases and atomic radius generally decreases; down a group, additional shells generally increase atomic radius. Trends have exceptions, so learn the explanations as well as the direction. Check yourself: Explain why elements in the same group often react similarly.","revision":[]},{"title":"4. Chemical Bonding","note":"Subtopics: ionic, covalent and metallic bonding, dot-and-cross diagrams, properties of compounds. Ionic bonding involves electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions after electron transfer. Covalent bonding involves shared electron pairs. Metallic bonding is attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons. Ionic substances often have high melting points and conduct electricity when molten or dissolved, but not as solids; simple molecular covalent substances often have lower melting points. Check yourself: Relate bonding and structure to conductivity and melting point.","revision":[]},{"title":"5. Chemical Formulae, Equations and Stoichiometry","note":"Subtopics: valency, formula writing, balancing equations, relative atomic/molecular mass, mole, reacting masses, limiting reagent. A chemical equation must conserve atoms and charge. Balance by changing coefficients, never subscripts. Relative molecular mass is the sum of relative atomic masses in a formula. Amount in moles: (n=m/M), where (m) is mass in grams and (M) is molar mass in g mol⁻¹. Use balanced equation coefficients as mole ratios. Check yourself: Balance an equation and calculate moles from mass and molar mass.","revision":[]},{"title":"6. States of Matter and Gas Laws","note":"Subtopics: kinetic theory, diffusion, melting and boiling, pressure, Boyle's law, Charles' law, ideal gas equation. Particles move continuously; temperature relates to average kinetic energy. At constant temperature, Boyle's law states (P_1V_1=P_2V_2). At constant pressure, Charles' law states (V_1/T_1=V_2/T_2), with temperature in kelvin. For an ideal gas, (PV=nRT) when units are consistent. Check yourself: Convert Celsius to kelvin before gas-law calculations.","revision":[]},{"title":"7. Solutions and Solubility","note":"Subtopics: solute, solvent, saturated solutions, concentration, solubility curves, dilution. A solution is a homogeneous mixture. Concentration in mol dm⁻³ is moles of solute divided by solution volume in dm³. Dilution reduces concentration without changing the amount of solute transferred: (C_1V_1=C_2V_2) for compatible units and a simple dilution. Solubility depends on substance and temperature; gas solubility often decreases as temperature rises. Check yourself: Convert cm³ to dm³ and calculate molar concentration.","revision":[]},{"title":"8. Acids, Bases and Salts","note":"Subtopics: acid and base definitions, pH, indicators, neutralisation, salt preparation, acidic/basic salts. Acids produce hydrogen ions in aqueous solution; bases neutralise acids, and alkalis are soluble bases that produce hydroxide ions. Neutralisation commonly forms salt and water. Use suitable methods to prepare salts: titration for soluble salts from acid and soluble alkali; excess insoluble base followed by filtration for many other soluble salts. Check yourself: Select an appropriate method for preparing a soluble salt.","revision":[]},{"title":"9. Oxidation and Reduction","note":"Subtopics: oxygen and hydrogen transfer, electron transfer, oxidation numbers, oxidising and reducing agents. Oxidation can mean electron loss or increase in oxidation number; reduction means electron gain or decrease in oxidation number. Oxidising agents accept electrons and are reduced; reducing agents donate electrons and are oxidised. In a redox reaction, oxidation and reduction occur together. Check yourself: Identify what is oxidised and reduced in a simple reaction.","revision":[]},{"title":"10. Electrolysis and Electrochemistry","note":"Subtopics: electrolytes, electrodes, cations and anions, discharge, electroplating, Faraday principles. Electrolysis uses electrical energy to drive a chemical change in an electrolyte. Cations move to the cathode and gain electrons; anions move to the anode and lose electrons. Products depend on ion concentration, electrode material and relative ease of discharge. In a simple electrochemical cell, chemical energy is converted to electrical energy. Check yourself: Predict electrode products only after considering the electrolyte and electrode type.","revision":[]},{"title":"11. Rates of Reaction","note":"Subtopics: collision theory, concentration, pressure, surface area, temperature, catalysts. A reaction occurs when particles collide with sufficient energy and suitable orientation. Higher temperature usually increases the fraction of particles with energy at or above activation energy. Greater concentration or gas pressure and larger solid surface area can increase collision frequency. Catalysts provide an alternative pathway with lower activation energy and are not consumed overall. Check yourself: Explain each rate factor using collision theory.","revision":[]},{"title":"12. Chemical Equilibrium","note":"Subtopics: reversible reactions, dynamic equilibrium, Le Chatelier's principle, temperature, pressure and concentration. At dynamic equilibrium in a closed system, forward and reverse reaction rates are equal and concentrations remain constant, though reactions continue. A system responds to a change in a way that tends to oppose it. Temperature changes can alter the equilibrium constant; catalysts speed both directions and do not change the equilibrium position. Check yourself: Predict how a change affects a simple reversible reaction.","revision":[]},{"title":"13. Energetics and Thermochemistry","note":"Subtopics: exothermic and endothermic reactions, enthalpy changes, bond energies, energy profiles. Exothermic reactions release heat to surroundings; endothermic reactions absorb heat. Breaking bonds requires energy; forming bonds releases energy. An approximate reaction enthalpy using bond energies is energy required to break bonds minus energy released on forming bonds. Check yourself: Interpret an energy profile and determine the sign of an enthalpy change.","revision":[]},{"title":"14. Water and Hydrogen","note":"Subtopics: properties of water, hardness, purification, hydrogen preparation and uses. Water is polar and forms hydrogen bonds, contributing to its relatively high boiling point. Temporary hardness is commonly caused by hydrogencarbonates and can be removed by boiling; permanent hardness commonly involves calcium or magnesium sulfates/chlorides and may require other treatment. Hydrogen is a light gas and burns in oxygen to form water. Check yourself: Compare temporary and permanent hardness.","revision":[]},{"title":"15. Air, Oxygen and Nitrogen","note":"Subtopics: air composition, oxygen preparation and uses, nitrogen, oxides, pollution. Air is mainly nitrogen and oxygen, with smaller amounts of argon, carbon dioxide and variable water vapour. Oxygen supports combustion but is not itself a fuel. Nitrogen is relatively unreactive at ordinary conditions because of the strong triple bond in N₂. Nitrogen oxides and sulfur dioxide contribute to air pollution and acid deposition. Check yourself: Explain why nitrogen is relatively unreactive and list sources of air pollution.","revision":[]},{"title":"16. Carbon and Its Compounds","note":"Subtopics: allotropes, hydrocarbons, homologous series, functional groups, isomerism. Diamond and graphite are carbon allotropes with different structures and properties. Hydrocarbons contain carbon and hydrogen only. Alkanes are saturated; alkenes contain a carbon–carbon double bond. Members of a homologous series share a functional group and general formula, with gradual changes in physical properties. Check yourself: Distinguish alkanes from alkenes using structure and a chemical test.","revision":[]},{"title":"17. Organic Nomenclature and Reactions","note":"Subtopics: naming simple organic compounds, combustion, substitution, addition, cracking, polymerisation. Identify the longest appropriate carbon chain and number it to give functional groups or substituents the lowest suitable locants. Alkanes commonly undergo substitution; alkenes undergo addition across the double bond. Complete combustion of hydrocarbons forms carbon dioxide and water; incomplete combustion can produce carbon monoxide or soot. Check yourself: Predict products of simple addition and combustion reactions.","revision":[]},{"title":"18. Petroleum and Petrochemicals","note":"Subtopics: crude oil, fractional distillation, cracking, reforming, fuels, pollution. Crude oil is a mixture of hydrocarbons. Fractional distillation separates fractions by boiling range rather than producing pure single compounds. Cracking breaks larger hydrocarbons into smaller, more useful molecules, including alkenes. Fuel choice involves energy output, availability, cost and environmental effects. Check yourself: Explain the difference between fractional distillation and cracking.","revision":[]},{"title":"19. Metals and Their Compounds","note":"Subtopics: reactivity series, extraction, corrosion, alloys, properties and uses. A metal's extraction method depends on its reactivity. Very reactive metals are commonly extracted by electrolysis; less reactive metals may be reduced from ores using carbon or other reducing agents. Rusting of iron requires oxygen and water; protective coatings, painting, galvanising and alloying can reduce corrosion. Check yourself: Relate the reactivity series to extraction and corrosion prevention.","revision":[]},{"title":"20. Non-metals and Their Compounds","note":"Subtopics: halogens, sulfur, phosphorus, chlorine, ammonia, oxides and common uses. Non-metals often form covalent compounds, though they can form ions in suitable reactions. Halogen reactivity generally decreases down Group 17. Ammonia is alkaline in water and is used in fertiliser manufacture. Chlorine can disinfect water, but must be handled carefully because it is toxic. Check yourself: Explain the displacement trend among halogens.","revision":[]},{"title":"21. Chemical Industries and Environmental Chemistry","note":"Subtopics: fertilisers, cement, glass, soap, water treatment, waste, greenhouse effect and acid rain. Industrial chemistry converts raw materials into useful products. Fertilisers supply plant nutrients; excessive use can cause eutrophication. Greenhouse gases absorb outgoing infrared radiation, while sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides can contribute to acid rain. Pollution control includes efficient processes, treatment of waste and responsible resource use. Check yourself: Link a pollutant to its source, effect and control.","revision":[]},{"title":"22. Qualitative Analysis","note":"Subtopics: gas tests, flame tests, tests for anions and cations, observations and inference. Tests must be reported as observation plus inference. Hydrogen gives a squeaky pop with a lit splint; oxygen relights a glowing splint; carbon dioxide turns limewater milky; ammonia turns damp red litmus blue. Use standard ion tests carefully and do not infer a compound from one inconclusive observation. Check yourself: Memorise gas tests and practise recording precise observations.","revision":[]},{"title":"23. Quantitative Chemistry and Laboratory Skills","note":"Subtopics: titration, standard solutions, apparatus, significant figures, graphs, safety and error. Read a burette at eye level and record initial and final readings. Concordant titres are close results suitable for averaging according to the practical instructions. Use correct units, repeat measurements where possible and distinguish precision from accuracy. Wear appropriate protection and never taste laboratory chemicals. Check yourself: Calculate titre, mean titre and concentration from a balanced equation.","revision":[]},{"title":"24. Nuclear Chemistry","note":"Subtopics: isotopes, radioactivity, alpha, beta and gamma radiation, half-life, applications and safety. Unstable nuclei emit radiation. Alpha particles are relatively strongly ionising and weakly penetrating; gamma radiation is less ionising and highly penetrating. Half-life is the time taken for the number of undecayed nuclei or activity to fall to half its initial value. Radiation has medical and industrial uses but requires careful control. Check yourself: Calculate remaining activity after whole half-life intervals.","revision":[]},{"title":"Revision Checklist","note":"• Practise formula writing, balancing equations, mole calculations and unit conversions. • Learn periodic trends, bonding-property links, gas tests and ion tests. • Explain rate and equilibrium changes rather than memorising predictions alone. • Compare every topic against the current official JAMB syllabus before treating this pack as exhaustive.","revision":["Practise formula writing, balancing equations, mole calculations and unit conversions.","Learn periodic trends, bonding-property links, gas tests and ion tests.","Explain rate and equilibrium changes rather than memorising predictions alone.","Compare every topic against the current official JAMB syllabus before treating this pack as exhaustive."]}],"questions":[{"q":"Which method is best for separating an insoluble solid from water?","o":["Filtration","Fractional distillation","Chromatography","Sublimation"],"a":0,"explanation":"Filtration retains insoluble solid while liquid passes through."},{"q":"Which method can separate coloured dyes in ink?","o":["Filtration","Chromatography","Decantation only","Crystallisation only"],"a":1,"explanation":"Chromatography separates dyes by their different movement."},{"q":"The atomic number of an element equals its number of","o":["neutrons","protons","protons plus neutrons","shells"],"a":1,"explanation":"Atomic number is proton number."},{"q":"Isotopes of an element have the same number of","o":["neutrons","nucleons","protons","protons and neutrons combined"],"a":2,"explanation":"Isotopes have equal proton numbers and different neutron numbers."},{"q":"Elements in the same group often have similar chemical properties because they have similar","o":["numbers of occupied shells","outer-shell electron arrangements","neutron numbers","atomic masses"],"a":1,"explanation":"Similar valence-shell arrangements produce related chemical behaviour."},{"q":"Across a period, atomic radius generally decreases mainly because","o":["nuclear charge increases","atoms lose all electrons","neutron number becomes zero","the nucleus disappears"],"a":0,"explanation":"Increasing nuclear attraction generally pulls electrons closer across a period."},{"q":"Ionic bonding is the electrostatic attraction between","o":["neutral molecules","oppositely charged ions","two neutrons","shared electron pairs only"],"a":1,"explanation":"Ionic bonding is attraction between oppositely charged ions."},{"q":"Solid sodium chloride does not conduct electricity well because its ions","o":["are absent","are fixed in a lattice","have no charge","become gases"],"a":1,"explanation":"The ions are not free to move in the solid lattice."},{"q":"When balancing a chemical equation, you should change","o":["subscripts in formulae","coefficients before formulae","element symbols","charges arbitrarily"],"a":1,"explanation":"Coefficients balance atom counts without changing chemical identities."},{"q":"How many moles are in 10 g of a substance with molar mass 20 g mol⁻¹?","o":["0.5 mol","2 mol","10 mol","200 mol"],"a":0,"explanation":"(n=m/M=10/20=0.5) mol."},{"q":"At constant temperature, Boyle's law relates pressure and volume by","o":["P/V = constant","PV = constant","P + V = constant","P = V²"],"a":1,"explanation":"For a fixed amount of gas at constant temperature, PV is constant."},{"q":"A temperature of 27°C is approximately","o":["27 K","246 K","300 K","373 K"],"a":2,"explanation":"Kelvin ≈ Celsius + 273, so 27°C ≈ 300 K."},{"q":"A solution contains 0.20 mol solute in 0.50 dm³ solution. Its concentration is","o":["0.10 mol dm⁻³","0.40 mol dm⁻³","2.5 mol dm⁻³","4.0 mol dm⁻³"],"a":1,"explanation":"Concentration = 0.20/0.50 = 0.40 mol dm⁻³."},{"q":"Which expression is commonly used for a simple dilution?","o":["C₁V₁ = C₂V₂","C₁ + V₁ = C₂","C/V = 1","C₁C₂ = V₁V₂"],"a":0,"explanation":"The relation accounts for the same amount of solute before and after dilution."},{"q":"Neutralisation of an acid by a base commonly produces","o":["salt and water","oxygen and hydrogen only","metal and carbon","glucose"],"a":0,"explanation":"Acid-base neutralisation commonly forms salt and water."},{"q":"Which solution is alkaline?","o":["One with pH 2","One with pH 7 exactly at 25°C","One with pH 11","One with pH 0"],"a":2,"explanation":"At about 25°C, pH above 7 is alkaline."},{"q":"Oxidation, in terms of electrons, is","o":["gain of electrons","loss of electrons","gain of neutrons","loss of protons from every atom"],"a":1,"explanation":"Oxidation is electron loss."},{"q":"An oxidising agent is a substance that","o":["accepts electrons and is reduced","donates electrons and is oxidised","never changes","stops all reactions"],"a":0,"explanation":"The oxidising agent accepts electrons and is reduced."},{"q":"During electrolysis, cations move towards the","o":["anode","cathode","salt bridge only","power switch"],"a":1,"explanation":"Positive cations are attracted to the negative cathode."},{"q":"A catalyst increases reaction rate mainly by","o":["raising activation energy","providing a lower-activation-energy pathway","being permanently consumed","changing all products"],"a":1,"explanation":"A catalyst provides a pathway with lower activation energy."},{"q":"Increasing temperature usually increases reaction rate because particles","o":["stop moving","collide more energetically and more may exceed activation energy","lose all kinetic energy","become atoms of a new element"],"a":1,"explanation":"Higher temperature increases particle kinetic energy and the effective collision fraction."},{"q":"At dynamic equilibrium in a closed system, the forward and reverse reaction rates are","o":["both zero","equal","always unequal","unrelated to concentration"],"a":1,"explanation":"Dynamic equilibrium has equal forward and reverse rates."},{"q":"A catalyst added to an equilibrium mixture generally","o":["changes the equilibrium constant","speeds attainment of equilibrium without changing its position","stops the reverse reaction","changes the balanced equation"],"a":1,"explanation":"A catalyst speeds both directions and does not alter equilibrium position."},{"q":"An exothermic reaction","o":["absorbs heat from surroundings","releases heat to surroundings","cannot occur spontaneously","always requires electricity"],"a":1,"explanation":"Exothermic reactions release heat."},{"q":"Breaking chemical bonds generally","o":["releases energy","requires energy","creates no energy change ever","removes all atoms"],"a":1,"explanation":"Energy is needed to overcome the attraction holding a bond together."},{"q":"Temporary hardness of water is commonly associated with","o":["calcium and magnesium hydrogencarbonates","sodium chloride only","pure oxygen","glucose"],"a":0,"explanation":"Temporary hardness commonly comes from hydrogencarbonates."},{"q":"Which gas relights a glowing splint?","o":["Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Ammonia","Hydrogen chloride"],"a":1,"explanation":"Oxygen relights a glowing splint."},{"q":"Nitrogen gas is relatively unreactive at room conditions partly because N₂ has","o":["a weak single bond","a strong triple bond","no electrons","an ionic lattice"],"a":1,"explanation":"The strong N≡N bond requires substantial energy to break."},{"q":"Which hydrocarbon is unsaturated?","o":["Ethane","Methane","Ethene","Propane"],"a":2,"explanation":"Ethene contains a carbon–carbon double bond."},{"q":"A homologous series has members with the same","o":["molecular mass","functional group and general formula","physical state at all temperatures","number of carbon atoms"],"a":1,"explanation":"Homologous members share a functional group and general formula."},{"q":"Complete combustion of a hydrocarbon produces","o":["carbon dioxide and water","carbon and hydrogen only","ammonia and salt","nitrogen and sulfur"],"a":0,"explanation":"Complete combustion forms carbon dioxide and water."},{"q":"Fractional distillation of crude oil separates fractions mainly by differences in","o":["colour only","boiling ranges","atomic number","acidity"],"a":1,"explanation":"Fractions condense over different boiling ranges."},{"q":"Cracking is used to","o":["join all small molecules into metals","break larger hydrocarbons into smaller molecules","remove all carbon from petroleum","turn water into crude oil"],"a":1,"explanation":"Cracking produces smaller hydrocarbons, often including alkenes."},{"q":"Rusting of iron requires","o":["oxygen and water","nitrogen alone","carbon dioxide alone in every case","sunlight only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Rusting requires both oxygen and water."},{"q":"Very reactive metals such as sodium are commonly extracted by","o":["simple filtration","electrolysis of suitable molten compounds","crystallising seawater","chromatography"],"a":1,"explanation":"Highly reactive metals are extracted by electrolysis rather than carbon reduction."},{"q":"Down Group 17, halogen reactivity generally","o":["increases","decreases","remains exactly identical","becomes zero immediately"],"a":1,"explanation":"Halogen reactivity generally falls down the group."},{"q":"Excess fertiliser entering a lake may cause","o":["eutrophication","elimination of all algae forever","no biological change","formation of pure oxygen only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Nutrient enrichment can cause algal growth and oxygen depletion."},{"q":"Which gas turns limewater milky?","o":["Hydrogen","Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Nitrogen"],"a":1,"explanation":"Carbon dioxide forms a milky calcium carbonate precipitate in limewater."},{"q":"Which gas turns damp red litmus paper blue?","o":["Ammonia","Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Hydrogen"],"a":0,"explanation":"Ammonia is alkaline in water and turns damp red litmus blue."},{"q":"A burette is commonly used to","o":["measure a variable volume accurately in titration","measure mass directly","heat a solid to fusion","determine atomic number"],"a":0,"explanation":"A burette delivers and measures volume precisely during titration."},{"q":"If a radioactive sample has an initial activity of 80 units, what remains after two half-lives?","o":["60 units","40 units","20 units","10 units"],"a":2,"explanation":"80 → 40 → 20 units after two half-lives."},{"q":"Which radiation is generally the most penetrating of alpha, beta and gamma?","o":["Alpha","Beta","Gamma","All are identical"],"a":2,"explanation":"Gamma radiation is generally the most penetrating."},{"q":"Which particle has a negative charge?","o":["Proton","Neutron","Electron","Alpha particle"],"a":2,"explanation":"Electrons carry negative charge."},{"q":"A salt can often be prepared from an acid and an insoluble base by adding excess base, then","o":["filtering and crystallising the solution","adding more insoluble base forever","discarding the liquid immediately","freezing the mixture only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Filtration removes excess insoluble base; crystallisation recovers the salt."},{"q":"Which statement about bond energies is correct?","o":["Bond breaking releases energy","Bond formation releases energy","Both always absorb energy","Neither involves energy"],"a":1,"explanation":"Bond formation releases energy; bond breaking requires energy."},{"q":"Which is a sensible laboratory safety practice?","o":["Taste unknown chemicals","Smell every gas directly","Wear suitable protection and follow instructions","Mix unknown reagents to see what happens"],"a":2,"explanation":"Protective equipment and safe procedures reduce risk."},{"q":"The formula of magnesium chloride is","o":["MgCl","MgCl₂","Mg₂Cl","Mg₂Cl₃"],"a":1,"explanation":"Mg²⁺ requires two Cl⁻ ions for a neutral compound."},{"q":"Which process converts a liquid into vapour from its surface below the boiling point?","o":["Freezing","Evaporation","Condensation","Sublimation"],"a":1,"explanation":"Evaporation occurs at a liquid's surface below its boiling point."}]},"Physics":{"lessons":[{"title":"1. Measurements and Units","note":"Subtopics: SI base and derived units, prefixes, significant figures, scalar and vector quantities, dimensions. Common SI base units include metre (m), kilogram (kg), second (s), ampere (A), kelvin (K) and mole (mol). Speed is scalar; velocity is vector. Derived units combine base units, for example force in newtons (N = kg m s⁻²). Convert units before calculations and report sensible significant figures. Check yourself: Distinguish mass from weight and scalar from vector.","revision":[]},{"title":"2. Position, Distance and Displacement","note":"Subtopics: reference point, distance, displacement, speed, velocity, acceleration, motion graphs. Distance is total path length; displacement is directed change in position. Average speed = distance/time; average velocity = displacement/time. Acceleration is change in velocity per time. The gradient of a displacement–time graph gives velocity; the gradient of a velocity–time graph gives acceleration, and area under it gives displacement. Check yourself: Interpret gradients and areas on motion graphs.","revision":[]},{"title":"3. Scalars, Vectors and Equilibrium","note":"Subtopics: vector addition, components, resultant, moments, equilibrium. Vectors have magnitude and direction. Perpendicular components can be combined using Pythagoras' theorem. For equilibrium, resultant force and resultant moment are zero. Moment = force × perpendicular distance from pivot. Check yourself: Resolve a vector into horizontal and vertical components.","revision":[]},{"title":"4. Newton's Laws and Momentum","note":"Subtopics: inertia, force, mass, acceleration, action–reaction, impulse, conservation of momentum. Newton's second law gives resultant force (F=ma) for constant mass. Action and reaction act on different bodies. Momentum (p=mv); impulse equals change in momentum. In an isolated system, total momentum is conserved during collisions. Check yourself: Solve a simple collision using momentum conservation.","revision":[]},{"title":"5. Work, Energy and Power","note":"Subtopics: work, kinetic and potential energy, conservation of energy, power, efficiency. Work done by a constant force along displacement is (W=Fscos\\theta). Kinetic energy is (E_k=\\frac12mv^2); gravitational potential energy near Earth's surface is (E_p=mgh). Power is work per time. Efficiency = useful output energy/input energy × 100%. Check yourself: Calculate energy changes and efficiency.","revision":[]},{"title":"6. Simple Machines","note":"Subtopics: levers, pulleys, inclined planes, mechanical advantage, velocity ratio, efficiency. Mechanical advantage = load/effort; velocity ratio = distance moved by effort/distance moved by load. Efficiency = MA/VR × 100%. Real machines lose energy, often through friction, so efficiency is below 100%. Check yourself: Explain why a machine can multiply force but not create energy.","revision":[]},{"title":"7. Elasticity and Properties of Matter","note":"Subtopics: density, pressure, Hooke's law, stress, strain, Young's modulus. Density = mass/volume. Pressure = force/area. Within the elastic limit, Hooke's law states extension is proportional to force. Stress = force/cross-sectional area; strain = extension/original length. Young's modulus = stress/strain. Check yourself: Use consistent units in density and pressure calculations.","revision":[]},{"title":"8. Fluids and Pressure","note":"Subtopics: liquid pressure, atmospheric pressure, Pascal's principle, buoyancy, Archimedes' principle, flotation. Pressure in a stationary liquid increases with depth: (p=\\rho gh) for gauge pressure. Upthrust equals the weight of displaced fluid. An object floats when its weight is balanced by upthrust. Hydraulic systems transmit pressure through enclosed fluids. Check yourself: Explain why an object can float in water.","revision":[]},{"title":"9. Heat and Temperature","note":"Subtopics: thermometers, thermal expansion, specific heat capacity, latent heat, conduction, convection, radiation. Temperature measures how hot or cold a body is; heat is energy transferred because of temperature difference. Heat energy for a temperature change is (Q=mc\\Delta T); during a phase change, (Q=mL). Conduction, convection and radiation transfer thermal energy by different mechanisms. Check yourself: Distinguish specific heat capacity from specific latent heat.","revision":[]},{"title":"10. Gas Laws and Kinetic Theory","note":"Subtopics: Boyle's law, Charles' law, pressure law, absolute temperature, molecular motion. Gas particles move randomly and collide with container walls, producing pressure. For fixed mass at constant temperature, (P_1V_1=P_2V_2). At constant pressure, (V/T) is constant; at constant volume, (P/T) is constant. Use kelvin for gas-law temperatures. Check yourself: Identify what stays constant in each gas law.","revision":[]},{"title":"11. Waves and Wave Motion","note":"Subtopics: transverse and longitudinal waves, wavelength, frequency, period, amplitude, wave speed, reflection and refraction. Waves transfer energy without net transport of matter. Wave speed (v=f\\lambda), and period (T=1/f). Transverse waves vibrate perpendicular to propagation; longitudinal waves vibrate parallel to it. Reflection changes direction at a boundary; refraction occurs when wave speed changes. Check yourself: Calculate wavelength from frequency and speed.","revision":[]},{"title":"12. Sound","note":"Subtopics: production, propagation, pitch, loudness, echo, resonance, ultrasound. Sound is a mechanical wave and needs a medium. Frequency relates to pitch; amplitude relates to loudness. An echo is reflected sound. Distance to a reflecting surface can be found from (d=vt/2) when the measured time is for the sound's round trip. Check yourself: Explain why sound cannot travel through a vacuum.","revision":[]},{"title":"13. Light and Geometrical Optics","note":"Subtopics: rectilinear propagation, reflection, refraction, mirrors, lenses, total internal reflection, optical instruments. Angle of incidence equals angle of reflection, measured from the normal. Refraction is bending caused by a change in speed. Refractive index (n=c/v). Convex lenses converge parallel rays; concave lenses diverge them. Ray diagrams help determine image position, nature and orientation. Check yourself: Draw ray diagrams for a plane mirror and a converging lens.","revision":[]},{"title":"14. Dispersion and Optical Phenomena","note":"Subtopics: prism, spectrum, colour, scattering, diffraction, interference and polarisation. White light can disperse into a spectrum because wavelengths refract by different amounts. Diffraction is spreading around edges or through openings; it is more noticeable when opening size is comparable to wavelength. Interference results from superposition of waves. Check yourself: Distinguish dispersion from diffraction.","revision":[]},{"title":"15. Electrostatics","note":"Subtopics: charge, conductors and insulators, charging, electric field, Coulomb's law, potential. Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. Charge is measured in coulombs. Coulomb force magnitude varies with the product of charges and inversely with square of separation. Electric field describes force per unit positive test charge. Check yourself: Explain charging by friction, contact and induction.","revision":[]},{"title":"16. Current Electricity","note":"Subtopics: current, potential difference, resistance, Ohm's law, series and parallel circuits, electrical power. Current (I=Q/t); potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge. Ohm's law (V=IR) applies to an ohmic conductor at constant physical conditions. Series resistances add; parallel resistors obey (1/R_p=1/R_1+1/R_2+\\dots). Power (P=VI=I^2R=V^2/R). Check yourself: Compare current and voltage in series and parallel circuits.","revision":[]},{"title":"17. Electrical Energy and Domestic Wiring","note":"Subtopics: electrical energy, kilowatt-hour, fuses, circuit breakers, earthing, safety. Electrical energy (E=Pt). One kilowatt-hour is the energy used by a 1 kW device in one hour. Fuses and circuit breakers interrupt excessive current; earthing provides a low-resistance safety path for fault current. Never handle exposed live wiring. Check yourself: Calculate energy cost from power, time and tariff.","revision":[]},{"title":"18. Magnetism and Electromagnetism","note":"Subtopics: magnetic fields, permanent magnets, current-carrying conductors, electromagnets, force on a conductor. Magnetic field lines run from north to south outside a magnet. Current produces a magnetic field. An electromagnet can be strengthened by increasing current or turns and by using a suitable core. A current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field can experience a force. Check yourself: State factors affecting electromagnet strength.","revision":[]},{"title":"19. Electromagnetic Induction and Transformers","note":"Subtopics: induced emf, Faraday's law, Lenz's law, generators, transformers, energy losses. Changing magnetic flux through a circuit induces emf. The induced effect opposes the change producing it. A transformer uses alternating current and mutual induction; for an ideal transformer (V_p/V_s=N_p/N_s). Step-up transformers raise voltage and step-down transformers lower it. Check yourself: Explain why a simple transformer does not operate with steady direct current.","revision":[]},{"title":"20. Atomic and Nuclear Physics","note":"Subtopics: atomic models, isotopes, radioactivity, alpha, beta and gamma radiation, half-life, fission and fusion. Radioactive decay is spontaneous and random. Alpha radiation is strongly ionising but weakly penetrating; gamma is highly penetrating. Half-life is the time for undecayed nuclei or activity to halve. Nuclear fission splits a heavy nucleus; fusion combines light nuclei. Check yourself: Calculate remaining activity after a number of half-lives.","revision":[]},{"title":"21. Electronics and Semiconductor Basics","note":"Subtopics: conductors, insulators, semiconductors, diodes, rectification, transistors, logic. Semiconductors have conductivity between conductors and insulators and can be controlled by doping. A diode conducts mainly in one direction and can rectify alternating current. Transistors can act as switches or amplifiers. Learn circuit symbols and the function of each component. Check yourself: Describe the role of a diode in a rectifier.","revision":[]},{"title":"22. Energy Resources and Environmental Physics","note":"Subtopics: renewable and non-renewable energy, solar, hydro, wind, fossil fuels, efficiency, pollution. Renewable sources replenish naturally on useful timescales; non-renewable fuels are finite. Compare energy sources by reliability, cost, efficiency, emissions and environmental impact. Energy cannot be created or destroyed, but useful energy becomes less available in real transfers. Check yourself: Compare advantages and limitations of two energy sources.","revision":[]},{"title":"23. Practical Physics and Data Analysis","note":"Subtopics: apparatus, measurements, tables, graphs, gradients, uncertainties, safety. Record units and instrument precision. Plot independent variable on the horizontal axis and dependent variable on the vertical axis. Use a large graph and a best-fit line when appropriate; gradient is change in vertical value divided by change in horizontal value. Repeat measurements and identify sources of uncertainty. Check yourself: Determine a gradient and explain how repeated measurements improve reliability.","revision":[]},{"title":"Revision Checklist","note":"• Memorise SI units, common equations and the meaning of each symbol. • Practise rearranging formulae and converting units before substituting values. • Draw circuit diagrams, ray diagrams and motion graphs. • Compare this pack against the current official JAMB syllabus before treating it as exhaustive.","revision":["Memorise SI units, common equations and the meaning of each symbol.","Practise rearranging formulae and converting units before substituting values.","Draw circuit diagrams, ray diagrams and motion graphs.","Compare this pack against the current official JAMB syllabus before treating it as exhaustive."]}],"questions":[{"q":"The SI unit of force is the","o":["joule","newton","watt","pascal"],"a":1,"explanation":"Force is measured in newtons."},{"q":"Which is a vector quantity?","o":["Mass","Time","Velocity","Temperature"],"a":2,"explanation":"Velocity has magnitude and direction."},{"q":"A runner covers 100 m in 20 s. Average speed is","o":["2 m/s","5 m/s","20 m/s","2000 m/s"],"a":1,"explanation":"Speed = 100/20 = 5 m/s."},{"q":"The gradient of a displacement–time graph represents","o":["acceleration","velocity","force","energy"],"a":1,"explanation":"Gradient is change in displacement per time."},{"q":"A 3 N force acts 2 m from a pivot perpendicularly. Its moment is","o":["1.5 Nm","5 Nm","6 Nm","9 Nm"],"a":2,"explanation":"Moment = force × perpendicular distance = 3 × 2 = 6 Nm."},{"q":"Newton's second law for constant mass is","o":["F=ma","P=IV","V=fλ","Q=mcΔT"],"a":0,"explanation":"Resultant force equals mass times acceleration for constant mass."},{"q":"Momentum is the product of","o":["mass and velocity","force and area","power and time","pressure and volume"],"a":0,"explanation":"Momentum p=mv."},{"q":"Work done by a force parallel to a 4 m displacement when the force is 5 N is","o":["0.8 J","9 J","20 J","25 J"],"a":2,"explanation":"Work = force × displacement = 5 × 4 = 20 J."},{"q":"The kinetic energy of a 2 kg object moving at 3 m/s is","o":["3 J","6 J","9 J","18 J"],"a":2,"explanation":"KE = ½ × 2 × 3² = 9 J."},{"q":"Efficiency is the ratio of","o":["input to wasted energy only","useful output to total input","mass to volume","force to acceleration"],"a":1,"explanation":"Efficiency compares useful output with total input."},{"q":"Mechanical advantage equals","o":["effort/load","load/effort","distance/time","power × time"],"a":1,"explanation":"Mechanical advantage = load/effort."},{"q":"Density is","o":["mass/volume","volume/mass","force/area","mass × acceleration"],"a":0,"explanation":"Density is mass divided by volume."},{"q":"Pressure at depth in a stationary liquid increases with","o":["decreasing depth only","depth","colour","container label"],"a":1,"explanation":"Liquid pressure increases with depth for a given liquid."},{"q":"Upthrust on an immersed object equals the","o":["object's volume only","weight of fluid displaced","object's temperature","surface area only"],"a":1,"explanation":"Archimedes' principle defines upthrust as displaced-fluid weight."},{"q":"Heat needed to raise the temperature of mass m by ΔT is","o":["Q=mcΔT","Q=m/c","Q=c/(mΔT)","Q=mv"],"a":0,"explanation":"This is the specific heat capacity relation."},{"q":"During melting of a pure substance, supplied energy is mainly used to","o":["increase temperature continuously","change state","reduce mass to zero","stop all particle motion"],"a":1,"explanation":"Latent heat changes state without raising temperature during the phase change."},{"q":"Which gas-law temperature scale must be used in calculations?","o":["Celsius only","Kelvin","Fahrenheit only","Any scale without conversion"],"a":1,"explanation":"Gas-law temperature must be absolute temperature in kelvin."},{"q":"A wave has frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 2 m. Its speed is","o":["2.5 m/s","7 m/s","10 m/s","25 m/s"],"a":2,"explanation":"v=fλ=5×2=10 m/s."},{"q":"Sound cannot travel through","o":["steel","water","air","vacuum"],"a":3,"explanation":"Sound needs a material medium."},{"q":"The pitch of a sound depends mainly on its","o":["frequency","speed alone","travel distance","medium colour"],"a":0,"explanation":"Higher frequency is heard as higher pitch."},{"q":"The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of","o":["refraction in every material","reflection","dispersion","diffraction"],"a":1,"explanation":"Reflection law equates incidence and reflection angles."},{"q":"Refractive index is defined as","o":["speed in medium/speed in vacuum","speed in vacuum/speed in medium","mass/volume","frequency × time"],"a":1,"explanation":"n=c/v for a medium."},{"q":"Diffraction is most noticeable when an opening is","o":["much larger than wavelength in every case","comparable in size to wavelength","completely closed","made of a magnet"],"a":1,"explanation":"Diffraction is strong when aperture size is similar to wavelength."},{"q":"Like electric charges","o":["always attract","repel","have no interaction","turn into neutrons"],"a":1,"explanation":"Like charges repel."},{"q":"Electric current is","o":["charge transferred per unit time","energy per unit mass","force per unit area","distance per unit time"],"a":0,"explanation":"Current I=Q/t."},{"q":"A 12 V potential difference across a 4 Ω resistor gives current","o":["0.33 A","3 A","8 A","48 A"],"a":1,"explanation":"I=V/R=12/4=3 A."},{"q":"In a series circuit, the current through each component is","o":["the same","always zero","different by definition","equal to resistance"],"a":0,"explanation":"Series components share the same current."},{"q":"Electrical power can be calculated using","o":["P=VI","P=V/I only","P=mgh","P=ρgh"],"a":0,"explanation":"Electrical power P=VI."},{"q":"One kilowatt-hour is a unit of","o":["current","energy","voltage","resistance"],"a":1,"explanation":"A kilowatt-hour is power multiplied by time, hence energy."},{"q":"The main purpose of a fuse is to","o":["increase voltage","interrupt excessive current","store charge permanently","produce magnetic poles"],"a":1,"explanation":"A fuse breaks the circuit if current becomes excessive."},{"q":"A current-carrying wire produces","o":["a magnetic field","no field ever","only gravity","nuclear fuel"],"a":0,"explanation":"Electric current creates a magnetic field."},{"q":"An electromagnet can be strengthened by","o":["reducing turns and current","increasing current or coil turns","removing its power source while operating","using only plastic as its core"],"a":1,"explanation":"More turns and current generally strengthen the field."},{"q":"Electromagnetic induction occurs when magnetic flux through a circuit","o":["changes","remains exactly constant always","is painted","has no relation to the circuit"],"a":0,"explanation":"Changing flux induces an emf."},{"q":"A transformer operates using","o":["steady direct current only","changing magnetic flux from alternating current","chemical digestion","static charge alone"],"a":1,"explanation":"Transformers require changing magnetic flux, normally supplied by AC."},{"q":"The most penetrating of alpha, beta and gamma radiation is generally","o":["alpha","beta","gamma","all equally penetrating"],"a":2,"explanation":"Gamma radiation is highly penetrating."},{"q":"After three half-lives, the fraction of a radioactive sample remaining is","o":["1/2","1/3","1/6","1/8"],"a":3,"explanation":"Remaining fraction is (1/2)³ = 1/8."},{"q":"Nuclear fission involves","o":["combining light nuclei","splitting a heavy nucleus","evaporating a liquid","separating mixtures"],"a":1,"explanation":"Fission splits a heavy nucleus."},{"q":"A diode is commonly used to","o":["allow current mainly in one direction","measure mass","increase gravity","store fuel"],"a":0,"explanation":"A diode conducts mainly in one direction."},{"q":"Which is a renewable energy source?","o":["Coal","Petroleum","Wind","Natural gas"],"a":2,"explanation":"Wind is naturally replenished."},{"q":"On a graph of y against x, the gradient is","o":["change in x/change in y","change in y/change in x","x+y","x multiplied by y"],"a":1,"explanation":"Gradient is rise divided by run."}]},"Agricultural Science":{"lessons":[{"title":"1. Meaning, Scope and Importance of Agriculture","note":"Subtopics: crop and animal production, food security, employment, raw materials, agricultural systems. Agriculture includes crop production, livestock management, fisheries, forestry and related services. It supplies food and industrial raw materials and supports jobs and rural livelihoods. Subsistence farming mainly supports a household; commercial farming is oriented towards sale and profit. Check yourself: Compare subsistence and commercial farming.","revision":[]},{"title":"2. Agricultural Ecology","note":"Subtopics: ecosystem, soil, climate, biotic and abiotic factors, ecological balance. Crop and livestock performance depends on light, rainfall, temperature, soil, organisms and management. Biotic factors include pests and beneficial organisms; abiotic factors include water, pH and temperature. Sustainable management seeks productivity without degrading the resource base. Check yourself: Identify limiting environmental factors for a crop.","revision":[]},{"title":"3. Soil Formation and Profile","note":"Subtopics: weathering, soil horizons, texture, structure, porosity, drainage. Soil forms through weathering of parent material and addition of organic matter. A soil profile contains horizons with different mineral and organic content. Sand drains quickly; clay holds more water but may drain poorly; loam often offers a useful balance for cultivation. Check yourself: Relate soil texture to water retention.","revision":[]},{"title":"4. Soil Fertility and Conservation","note":"Subtopics: macro- and micronutrients, manure, compost, fertilisers, erosion control, crop rotation. Plants need nutrients such as nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium. Organic manure adds organic matter; fertilisers supply specified nutrients. Crop rotation, cover crops, mulching, contour farming and terracing can help reduce erosion and maintain soil quality. Check yourself: Match common nutrient deficiencies to plant symptoms using a reliable reference.","revision":[]},{"title":"5. Land Preparation and Tillage","note":"Subtopics: clearing, ploughing, harrowing, ridging, tools and machinery. Land preparation creates suitable conditions for planting. Tillage can loosen soil and control weeds but excessive tillage can increase erosion and moisture loss. Select tools according to scale, crop and soil conditions. Check yourself: Distinguish ploughing from harrowing.","revision":[]},{"title":"6. Crop Propagation and Planting","note":"Subtopics: seeds, viability, germination, nursery practices, vegetative propagation, spacing. Crops may be propagated by seed or vegetative methods such as cuttings, grafting and suckers. Germination requires viable seed and suitable water, oxygen and temperature. Correct spacing reduces competition and supports air circulation. Check yourself: Give examples of crops propagated by seed and vegetative methods.","revision":[]},{"title":"7. Crop Husbandry","note":"Subtopics: planting, thinning, weeding, mulching, staking, pruning, irrigation and harvesting. Good husbandry matches operations to crop growth stage. Weeds compete for resources; mulching conserves moisture and can suppress weeds. Harvest at suitable maturity to protect quality and reduce losses. Check yourself: Explain why timing matters for weeding and harvesting.","revision":[]},{"title":"8. Crop Classification and Uses","note":"Subtopics: cereals, legumes, roots and tubers, vegetables, fruits, oil crops, fibre crops. Cereals such as maize provide carbohydrate; legumes such as cowpea can enrich soil through symbiosis with nitrogen-fixing bacteria; roots and tubers store energy. Crop choice depends on climate, soil, market and intended use. Check yourself: Classify common Nigerian crops by group and use.","revision":[]},{"title":"9. Crop Pests and Diseases","note":"Subtopics: pest types, symptoms, disease agents, cultural, biological and chemical control. Pests damage crops directly or transmit disease. Disease may be caused by fungi, bacteria, viruses or other agents. Integrated pest management combines monitoring, cultural methods, biological controls and careful use of approved pesticides. Check yourself: Distinguish a pest from a disease pathogen.","revision":[]},{"title":"10. Weeds and Weed Control","note":"Subtopics: weed identification, competition, life cycles, manual, cultural and chemical control. Weeds compete with crops for light, nutrients, water and space. Control options include hand weeding, mulching, crop rotation and selective herbicides used according to label directions. Preventing seed production can reduce future weed pressure. Check yourself: Explain two non-chemical weed-control methods.","revision":[]},{"title":"11. Agricultural Machinery and Farm Tools","note":"Subtopics: hand tools, tractors, planters, harvesters, maintenance and safety. Mechanisation can improve timeliness and reduce labour demands. Machinery must match farm size and crop conditions. Routine maintenance, guarding, training and safe operation reduce accidents and downtime. Check yourself: Match tools to farm operations.","revision":[]},{"title":"12. Irrigation and Drainage","note":"Subtopics: water sources, surface, sprinkler and drip irrigation, drainage, water conservation. Irrigation supplies water when rainfall is insufficient; drainage removes excess water. Drip systems can deliver water near roots with reduced evaporation, while surface systems may be less expensive but can lose more water. Poor irrigation management may cause waterlogging or salinity. Check yourself: Compare irrigation methods by cost and water efficiency.","revision":[]},{"title":"13. Farm Animals and Their Classification","note":"Subtopics: ruminants, non-ruminants, poultry, fish, breeds and production purpose. Livestock include cattle, sheep, goats, pigs, poultry and fish. Ruminants have a multi-compartment stomach and microbial fermentation; poultry and pigs are non-ruminants. Choose breeds for climate adaptation, disease resistance and production goals. Check yourself: Compare the digestive systems of ruminants and poultry.","revision":[]},{"title":"14. Animal Nutrition","note":"Subtopics: carbohydrates, proteins, fats, minerals, vitamins, water, ration and feed formulation. Feeds provide energy, protein, minerals and vitamins. A balanced ration meets the animal's needs for maintenance, growth, reproduction or production. Ruminants use rumen microbes to digest cellulose more effectively than non-ruminants. Check yourself: Explain why young and lactating animals may need different rations.","revision":[]},{"title":"15. Animal Reproduction and Breeding","note":"Subtopics: reproductive systems, oestrus, mating, artificial insemination, selection, crossbreeding. Breeding programmes aim to improve useful inherited traits while maintaining health and adaptability. Selection chooses breeding animals with desired traits; crossbreeding combines characteristics of different breeds. Responsible management considers genetic diversity and welfare. Check yourself: Distinguish selection from crossbreeding.","revision":[]},{"title":"16. Animal Health and Disease Control","note":"Subtopics: parasites, infectious diseases, vaccination, quarantine, hygiene, biosecurity. Disease may be infectious, parasitic, nutritional or environmental. Biosecurity reduces the introduction and spread of pathogens through hygiene, isolation of sick animals and controlled movement. Follow veterinary guidance for diagnosis, vaccines and medicines. Check yourself: Explain how quarantine protects a herd.","revision":[]},{"title":"17. Livestock Management and Housing","note":"Subtopics: housing, ventilation, stocking density, sanitation, handling, welfare. Housing should protect animals from weather and predators while allowing ventilation, drainage and suitable space. Overcrowding raises stress and disease risk. Clean water, proper feed and humane handling are essential. Check yourself: List features of a suitable poultry house.","revision":[]},{"title":"18. Fisheries and Aquaculture","note":"Subtopics: capture fisheries, fish ponds, stocking, feeding, water quality, harvesting. Aquaculture raises aquatic organisms under managed conditions. Water quality, oxygen, stocking density, feed and disease monitoring influence yield. Overfishing and habitat damage threaten wild fish populations. Check yourself: State factors that affect fish pond productivity.","revision":[]},{"title":"19. Forestry and Agroforestry","note":"Subtopics: forest products, afforestation, reforestation, agroforestry, conservation. Forests provide timber, fuel, habitat and ecosystem services. Afforestation establishes trees on land not recently forested; reforestation restores tree cover. Agroforestry integrates trees with crops or livestock and may improve shade, soil protection and biodiversity. Check yourself: Distinguish afforestation from reforestation.","revision":[]},{"title":"20. Agricultural Economics and Farm Records","note":"Subtopics: fixed and variable costs, revenue, profit, budgeting, depreciation, records. Farm records support decisions and accountability. Profit = total revenue − total cost. Fixed costs do not vary directly with output over a short period; variable costs change with production level. Budgets estimate expected income and expenditure. Check yourself: Calculate profit from given costs and sales.","revision":[]},{"title":"21. Agricultural Marketing and Cooperatives","note":"Subtopics: marketing channels, storage, transport, pricing, grading, cooperatives. Marketing moves produce from farms to consumers. Poor roads, inadequate storage and price instability can increase post-harvest losses. Cooperatives may improve members' bargaining power and access to inputs or markets. Check yourself: Identify stages in a crop's route to market.","revision":[]},{"title":"22. Agricultural Extension and Rural Development","note":"Subtopics: extension methods, demonstrations, farmer groups, communication, adoption of innovation. Extension services share practical information and support farmers' decisions. Demonstrations, farm visits, group meetings and radio can communicate techniques. Recommendations should be adapted to local conditions and evaluated. Check yourself: Explain how extension helps farmers adopt improved practices.","revision":[]},{"title":"23. Post-Harvest Handling and Storage","note":"Subtopics: drying, sorting, grading, storage pests, processing, preservation. Drying reduces moisture and can limit microbial growth. Proper storage protects produce from pests, dampness and contamination. Processing can add value, but hygiene and safe handling are necessary throughout the supply chain. Check yourself: Give methods to reduce grain storage losses.","revision":[]},{"title":"24. Agricultural Policy, Challenges and Sustainability","note":"Subtopics: credit, land tenure, labour, climate change, food security, sustainable practices. Farmers may face constraints involving finance, land access, infrastructure, inputs, markets and climate variability. Sustainable agriculture combines productivity with soil and water protection, biodiversity, economic viability and responsible resource use. Check yourself: Suggest practical responses to two local agricultural challenges.","revision":[]},{"title":"Revision Checklist","note":"• Learn crop groups, farm tools, soil conservation and nutrient functions. • Practise farm calculations involving profit, costs, yields and simple records. • Distinguish livestock classes, feeding, breeding and disease-control methods. • Verify topic completeness against the current official JAMB syllabus.","revision":["Learn crop groups, farm tools, soil conservation and nutrient functions.","Practise farm calculations involving profit, costs, yields and simple records.","Distinguish livestock classes, feeding, breeding and disease-control methods.","Verify topic completeness against the current official JAMB syllabus."]}],"questions":[{"q":"Farming mainly to provide food for a household is called","o":["commercial farming","subsistence farming","plantation export farming","mechanised processing"],"a":1,"explanation":"Subsistence farming primarily meets household needs."},{"q":"Which is an abiotic factor affecting crops?","o":["Insect pest","Fungal pathogen","Rainfall","Earthworm"],"a":2,"explanation":"Rainfall is a non-living environmental factor."},{"q":"Soil is formed partly through","o":["weathering of parent material","photosynthesis alone","evaporation of metal","animal respiration only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Weathering breaks down parent material during soil formation."},{"q":"Which soil type generally drains water fastest?","o":["Clay","Sand","Heavy waterlogged clay","Pure humus only"],"a":1,"explanation":"Sand has larger particles and generally drains rapidly."},{"q":"Which nutrient is a major component of proteins and is commonly supplied by nitrate fertilisers?","o":["Nitrogen","Sodium","Lead","Helium"],"a":0,"explanation":"Nitrogen is needed to make amino acids and proteins."},{"q":"Which practice helps reduce soil erosion on slopes?","o":["Up-and-down-slope tillage","Contour farming","Removing all vegetation","Burning all crop residues every season"],"a":1,"explanation":"Contour farming slows runoff on slopes."},{"q":"Harrowing is generally carried out to","o":["break soil clods and prepare a finer seedbed","milk cattle","harvest fish","vaccinate poultry"],"a":0,"explanation":"Harrowing breaks clods and refines the seedbed."},{"q":"Which is a vegetative propagation method?","o":["Seed dispersal","Grafting","Pollination only","Fertilisation"],"a":1,"explanation":"Grafting joins plant parts for vegetative propagation."},{"q":"Mulching can help to","o":["conserve soil moisture","guarantee no weeds ever grow","remove all nutrients","prevent root growth"],"a":0,"explanation":"Mulch reduces evaporation and may suppress weeds."},{"q":"Cowpea belongs to which crop group?","o":["Legumes","Cereals","Fibre crops only","Beverage crops only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Cowpea is a legume."},{"q":"Integrated pest management combines","o":["only repeated pesticide use","monitoring and multiple appropriate control methods","no observation","burning all farmland"],"a":1,"explanation":"Integrated pest management uses monitoring and suitable combined methods."},{"q":"Weeds reduce crop yield mainly by","o":["competing for resources","producing all crop nutrients","increasing crop spacing","stopping rainfall"],"a":0,"explanation":"Weeds compete with crops for light, water, nutrients and space."},{"q":"A tractor is primarily used to","o":["provide power for farm operations","measure soil pH only","incubate eggs only","test milk fat only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Tractors power or pull implements for farm operations."},{"q":"Drip irrigation delivers water mainly","o":["near plant roots through emitters","by flooding every road","only as vapour","through crop leaves with no equipment"],"a":0,"explanation":"Drip systems deliver water near the root zone."},{"q":"Which animal is a ruminant?","o":["Cattle","Chicken","Pig","Tilapia"],"a":0,"explanation":"Cattle have a ruminant digestive system."},{"q":"A balanced ration supplies nutrients in amounts that","o":["meet the animal's needs","contain protein only","exclude water","are identical for every animal and stage"],"a":0,"explanation":"Rations are formulated for the animal's particular needs."},{"q":"Crossbreeding involves","o":["mating animals of different breeds","selecting only one animal without mating","storing feed","treating soil"],"a":0,"explanation":"Crossbreeding mates animals from different breeds."},{"q":"Quarantine helps livestock health by","o":["isolating new or potentially infected animals","mixing all sick animals with healthy ones","removing clean water","stopping vaccination"],"a":0,"explanation":"Isolation can prevent disease introduction and spread."},{"q":"Good livestock housing should provide","o":["poor ventilation","suitable space, drainage and ventilation","permanent dampness","no access to clean water"],"a":1,"explanation":"Space, drainage and ventilation support health and welfare."},{"q":"Fish pond productivity is affected by","o":["water quality and dissolved oxygen","paint colour of the farmer's house","road signs only","moonlight alone"],"a":0,"explanation":"Water quality and oxygen strongly affect fish survival and growth."},{"q":"Reforestation means","o":["restoring tree cover to previously forested land","removing all trees","draining fish ponds","applying fertiliser to concrete"],"a":0,"explanation":"Reforestation restores forest cover where it existed before."},{"q":"Farm profit is","o":["total cost minus revenue","revenue minus total cost","revenue plus all debts","yield divided by rainfall"],"a":1,"explanation":"Profit is revenue less total cost."},{"q":"A variable cost may include","o":["seed purchased for each production cycle","land purchase price only","a permanent building's original cost only","the farmer's family name"],"a":0,"explanation":"Seeds are a production input whose cost changes with the crop cycle and scale."},{"q":"Cooperatives may help farmers by","o":["improving collective bargaining and access to services","preventing all market changes","guaranteeing unlimited rainfall","ending the need for records"],"a":0,"explanation":"Collective action may improve access and negotiating power."},{"q":"Drying grains before safe storage helps to","o":["reduce moisture that supports mould","increase water content","make all pests harmless instantly","remove the need for storage hygiene"],"a":0,"explanation":"Lower moisture reduces mould risk when grains are stored properly."},{"q":"Which is an agricultural extension method?","o":["Farm demonstration","Hiding all new information","Deliberately spreading misinformation","Avoiding farmer communication"],"a":0,"explanation":"Demonstrations let farmers observe techniques in practice."},{"q":"Which practice can improve soil organic matter?","o":["Adding suitable compost","Removing all residues and organic matter permanently","Repeated erosion","Dumping plastics"],"a":0,"explanation":"Compost adds organic matter and nutrients."},{"q":"A disease caused by a pathogen is best managed by","o":["correct identification and appropriate prevention or treatment","guessing medicine doses","ignoring biosecurity","withholding clean water"],"a":0,"explanation":"Effective control depends on correct diagnosis and suitable action."},{"q":"Which crop is commonly classified as a cereal?","o":["Maize","Cowpea","Cassava","Cocoa"],"a":0,"explanation":"Maize is a cereal grain."},{"q":"The main purpose of farm records is to","o":["support planning and evaluate performance","replace all practical work","guarantee profit regardless of conditions","prevent measuring costs"],"a":0,"explanation":"Records support budgeting, decision-making and review."}]},"Further Mathematics":{"lessons":[{"title":"1. Functions and Relations","note":"A relation pairs members of one set with members of another. A function assigns exactly one output to each allowed input. The domain is the set of inputs; the range is the set of outputs actually produced. For example, if f(x)=2x+3, then f(4)=11. Remember: Different inputs may have the same output, but one input cannot have two outputs in a function.","revision":[]},{"title":"2. Polynomial Equations","note":"A polynomial is an expression such as ax²+bx+c. Its degree is the highest power with a non-zero coefficient. To solve a polynomial equation, factor it where possible and use the zero-product rule. For x²−5x+6=0, factor to (x−2)(x−3)=0, so x=2 or x=3. Check: Substitute each root into the original equation.","revision":[]},{"title":"3. Complex Numbers","note":"A complex number has the form a+bi, where i²=−1. The real part is a and the imaginary part is b. Add and subtract by collecting like parts. Multiply using expansion and replace i² with −1. The conjugate of a+bi is a−bi. Example: (2+3i)+(4−i)=6+2i.","revision":[]},{"title":"4. Mathematical Induction","note":"Mathematical induction proves a statement for all positive integers in a sequence. First verify the base case, usually n=1. Then assume the statement is true for n=k and use that assumption to prove it for n=k+1. Both stages are essential. Common error: Proving only the base case does not prove the whole statement.","revision":[]},{"title":"5. Binomial Theorem","note":"For a non-negative integer n, (a+b)^n can be expanded using binomial coefficients: (a+b)^n=Σ C(n,r)a^(n−r)b^r. The coefficients in row n of Pascal’s triangle give the same coefficients. The general term helps find a particular power without expanding every term. Example: (x+2)²=x²+4x+4.","revision":[]},{"title":"6. Permutations and Combinations","note":"A permutation counts arrangements where order matters: nPr=n!/(n−r)!. A combination counts selections where order does not matter: nCr=n!/[r!(n−r)!]. Factorial means n!=n(n−1)…1, with 0!=1. Tip: Seating people is usually an arrangement; choosing a committee is usually a selection.","revision":[]},{"title":"7. Matrices and Determinants","note":"A matrix is a rectangular arrangement of entries. Addition requires equal dimensions. Matrix multiplication is possible when the first matrix’s column count equals the second matrix’s row count. For a 2×2 matrix [[a,b],[c,d]], the determinant is ad−bc. Example: det([[2,1],[3,4]])=8−3=5.","revision":[]},{"title":"8. Vectors in Two and Three Dimensions","note":"A vector has magnitude and direction and may be written in component form. Add vectors component by component. The magnitude of (a,b) is √(a²+b²); in three dimensions, the magnitude of (a,b,c) is √(a²+b²+c²). The dot product is a·b=a₁b₁+a₂b₂(+a₃b₃). Use: Vectors describe displacement, velocity and forces.","revision":[]},{"title":"9. Coordinate Geometry","note":"The gradient of a line through (x₁,y₁) and (x₂,y₂) is m=(y₂−y₁)/(x₂−x₁), when x₂≠x₁. A line can be written y−y₁=m(x−x₁). Parallel non-vertical lines have equal gradients; perpendicular non-vertical lines have gradients whose product is −1. Example: The gradient between (1,2) and (3,8) is 3.","revision":[]},{"title":"10. Conic Sections","note":"Conic sections include circles, parabolas, ellipses and hyperbolas. A circle with centre (h,k) and radius r has equation (x−h)²+(y−k)²=r². A parabola can be described as points equidistant from a focus and a directrix. Identify a conic by its standard equation and key features. Study focus: Centre, radius, focus, directrix, axes and vertices where applicable.","revision":[]},{"title":"11. Sequences and Series","note":"A sequence is an ordered list; a series is the sum of its terms. An arithmetic progression has common difference d and nth term aₙ=a+(n−1)d. Its first n terms sum to Sₙ=n/2[2a+(n−1)d]. A geometric progression has common ratio r and nth term ar^(n−1). Example: In 3, 7, 11, … the common difference is 4.","revision":[]},{"title":"12. Limits and Continuity","note":"A limit describes the value a function approaches as x approaches a point. For polynomials, direct substitution often evaluates a limit. If substitution gives 0/0, factorise or simplify before substituting again. A function is continuous at a point when its value exists there and equals the limit from both sides. Caution: A limit may exist even when the function is undefined at the point.","revision":[]},{"title":"13. Differentiation","note":"Differentiation gives the instantaneous rate of change or gradient of a curve. The power rule is d(xⁿ)/dx=nxⁿ⁻¹. Constants differentiate to zero, and constant multiples can be taken outside the derivative. Example: d(3x²−4x+7)/dx=6x−4.","revision":[]},{"title":"14. Applications of Differentiation","note":"The derivative gives the gradient of a tangent. At a stationary point, dy/dx=0. The second derivative can help classify a stationary point: if y''>0, the curve has a local minimum; if y''<0, it has a local maximum, subject to the usual conditions. Derivatives also model velocity and acceleration from displacement. Method: Differentiate, solve y'=0, then test the result and interpret the context.","revision":[]},{"title":"15. Integration","note":"Integration reverses differentiation and calculates accumulated quantities. The power rule is ∫xⁿ dx=xⁿ⁺¹/(n+1)+C for n≠−1. The constant C is required for an indefinite integral. A definite integral evaluates an antiderivative at the upper limit minus its value at the lower limit. Example: ∫2x dx=x²+C.","revision":[]},{"title":"16. Differential Equations","note":"A differential equation relates a function to one or more of its derivatives. A simple separable equation can often be solved by placing terms involving y on one side and x on the other, then integrating both sides. Use an initial condition to determine the integration constant. Check: Differentiate your proposed solution and substitute it back into the equation.","revision":[]},{"title":"17. Numerical Methods","note":"Numerical methods approximate solutions when an exact algebraic solution is difficult. Iteration repeatedly applies a rule to improve an estimate. In interval methods such as bisection, choose an interval where a continuous function changes sign, then halve the interval repeatedly. Good practice: Keep sufficient decimal places during working and round only at the end.","revision":[]},{"title":"18. Probability Distributions","note":"A random variable assigns numerical values to outcomes. For a discrete distribution, probabilities are non-negative and their total is 1. The expected value is E(X)=ΣxP(X=x). For a binomial distribution, P(X=r)=C(n,r)p^r(1−p)^(n−r), when trials are independent and have constant success probability p. Remember: State the assumptions before applying a distribution formula.","revision":[]},{"title":"19. Statistics and Correlation","note":"The mean is the arithmetic average; the median is the middle value after ordering; the mode is the most frequent value. Variance measures spread around the mean and standard deviation is its square root. Correlation describes the strength and direction of a linear association, but correlation alone does not prove causation. Interpretation: A correlation near +1 is strong positive linear association; near −1 is strong negative association.","revision":[]},{"title":"20. Mechanics","note":"Mechanics applies mathematics to motion and forces. For constant acceleration, v=u+at, s=ut+½at² and v²=u²+2as. Newton’s second law is F=ma. Weight is W=mg; momentum is p=mv. Always choose a consistent sign convention and SI units. Worked example: A 2 kg object accelerating at 3 m/s² has resultant force F=2×3=6 N.","revision":[]},{"title":"Quick Revision Checklist","note":"• Distinguish permutations from combinations. • Practise complex-number operations and matrix determinants. • Memorise and apply differentiation and integration rules. • Interpret gradients, stationary points and definite integrals. • Use probability formulas only when their assumptions fit. • Show full working, units and sensible rounding in mechanics problems.","revision":["Distinguish permutations from combinations.","Practise complex-number operations and matrix determinants.","Memorise and apply differentiation and integration rules.","Interpret gradients, stationary points and definite integrals.","Use probability formulas only when their assumptions fit.","Show full working, units and sensible rounding in mechanics problems."]}],"questions":[{"q":"If f(x)=3x−2, find f(4).","o":["8","10","12","14"],"a":1,"explanation":"f(4)=3(4)−2=10."},{"q":"Which statement defines a function?","o":["Every input has exactly one output","Every output has two inputs","Inputs must be negative","Every relation is a function ## Polynomial Equations"],"a":0,"explanation":"A function gives exactly one output for every input in its domain."},{"q":"Solve x²−7x+12=0.","o":["2 and 6","3 and 4","−3 and −4","1 and 12"],"a":1,"explanation":"(x−3)(x−4)=0, so x=3 or 4."},{"q":"What is the degree of 5x⁴−2x²+1?","o":["1","2","3","4 ## Complex Numbers"],"a":3,"explanation":"The highest power is 4."},{"q":"Simplify (3+2i)+(1−5i).","o":["4−3i","4+7i","2−3i","3−5i"],"a":0,"explanation":"Add real parts and imaginary parts: 4−3i."},{"q":"Given i²=−1, what is (2i)²?","o":["4","−4","2i","−2i ## Mathematical Induction and Binomial Theorem"],"a":1,"explanation":"(2i)²=4i²=−4."},{"q":"What must be done first in a standard proof by induction?","o":["Assume every case","Verify the base case","Differentiate","Find a determinant"],"a":1,"explanation":"The base case starts the induction proof."},{"q":"The coefficient of x in (x+2)³ is","o":["2","4","8","12 ## Permutations and Combinations"],"a":2,"explanation":"(x+2)³ includes 3x(2²)=12x; therefore the correct coefficient is 12, option D."},{"q":"How many ways can 3 different books be arranged on a shelf?","o":["3","6","9","12"],"a":1,"explanation":"3!=6 arrangements."},{"q":"How many ways can 2 students be selected from 5 students?","o":["5","10","15","20 ## Matrices and Vectors"],"a":1,"explanation":"5C2=5!/(2!3!)=10."},{"q":"Find the determinant of [[2,1],[3,4]].","o":["2","5","8","11"],"a":1,"explanation":"Determinant=2×4−1×3=5."},{"q":"Find the magnitude of vector (3,4).","o":["4","5","7","12 ## Coordinate Geometry and Conics"],"a":1,"explanation":"√(3²+4²)=5."},{"q":"Find the gradient between (1,2) and (3,8).","o":["2","3","4","6"],"a":1,"explanation":"(8−2)/(3−1)=3."},{"q":"A circle has centre (0,0) and radius 3. Its equation is","o":["x+y=3","x²+y²=3","x²+y²=9","x²−y²=9 ## Sequences and Series"],"a":2,"explanation":"x²+y²=r²=9."},{"q":"Find the 6th term of 2, 5, 8, 11, …","o":["14","15","17","20"],"a":2,"explanation":"Common difference 3; 6th term=2+5×3=17."},{"q":"Find the common ratio of 3, 6, 12, 24, …","o":["2","3","4","6 ## Limits and Differentiation"],"a":0,"explanation":"Each term is multiplied by 2."},{"q":"Evaluate lim(x→2)(x²+1).","o":["3","4","5","8"],"a":2,"explanation":"Substitute x=2: 2²+1=5."},{"q":"Differentiate y=4x³.","o":["4x²","7x²","12x²","12x³"],"a":2,"explanation":"By the power rule, dy/dx=12x²."},{"q":"If y=x²−4x, the stationary points occur when x is","o":["0","2","4","−2 ## Integration and Differential Equations"],"a":1,"explanation":"dy/dx=2x−4=0 gives x=2."},{"q":"Evaluate ∫3x² dx.","o":["x³+C","6x+C","3x³+C","x²+C"],"a":0,"explanation":"The antiderivative of 3x² is x³+C."},{"q":"If dy/dx=2x and y=1 when x=0, then y equals","o":["x²","x²+1","2x+1","x²−1 ## Probability and Statistics"],"a":1,"explanation":"Integrate to get y=x²+C; y(0)=1 gives C=1."},{"q":"A fair coin is tossed twice. What is the probability of two heads?","o":["1/2","1/3","1/4","3/4"],"a":2,"explanation":"Outcomes HH, HT, TH, TT are equally likely; one of four is HH."},{"q":"Find the mean of 2, 4, 6, 8.","o":["4","5","6","8"],"a":1,"explanation":"(2+4+6+8)/4=5."},{"q":"A correlation coefficient of −0.9 indicates","o":["Strong negative linear association","Strong positive linear association","No linear association exactly","Proof of causation ## Mechanics"],"a":0,"explanation":"−0.9 indicates a strong negative linear association, not causation."},{"q":"A 4 kg object accelerates at 2 m/s². Find the resultant force.","o":["2 N","4 N","6 N","8 N"],"a":3,"explanation":"F=ma=4×2=8 N."},{"q":"A body starts from rest and accelerates at 3 m/s² for 4 s. Its final speed is","o":["7 m/s","12 m/s","16 m/s","24 m/s"],"a":1,"explanation":"v=u+at=0+3×4=12 m/s."}]},"Technical Drawing":{"lessons":[{"title":"1. Drawing Instruments and Conventions","note":"Common instruments include drawing board, T-square, set squares, scale rule, compass, divider, protractor, pencils and eraser. Use sharp pencils, clean paper and light construction lines. Line types communicate meaning: visible outlines are thick continuous lines; hidden edges are dashed; centre lines use a chain pattern. Follow the conventions specified in the question.","revision":[]},{"title":"2. Geometrical Construction","note":"Geometrical construction uses instruments to create accurate lines, angles, perpendiculars, bisectors and shapes. A perpendicular bisector divides a line segment into two equal parts at 90°. An angle bisector divides an angle into two equal angles. Leave construction arcs visible when required to show the method.","revision":[]},{"title":"3. Angles, Polygons and Circles","note":"A polygon is a closed plane figure made from straight segments. The sum of interior angles of an n-sided polygon is (n−2)×180°. A regular polygon has equal sides and equal angles. A circle is defined by points at a constant distance from its centre; diameter=2×radius.","revision":[]},{"title":"4. Loci","note":"A locus is the path traced by a point satisfying a condition. Points at a fixed distance from one point form a circle. Points equidistant from two fixed points lie on the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining them. Points equidistant from two intersecting lines lie on their angle bisectors.","revision":[]},{"title":"5. Scales and Dimensioning","note":"A scale relates drawing size to actual size. A 1:100 scale means 1 unit on the drawing represents 100 of the same units in reality. Dimension lines, extension lines, arrowheads and figures should be clear and not ambiguous. Convert units consistently before measuring or calculating.","revision":[]},{"title":"6. Orthographic Projection","note":"Orthographic projection represents a 3D object through 2D views projected perpendicular to projection planes. Front, plan (top) and end views show different dimensions. Align views correctly and project features between views. Hidden details may be shown with dashed lines when needed.","revision":[]},{"title":"7. Isometric and Oblique Drawing","note":"An isometric drawing shows three principal axes equally spaced, conventionally with two receding axes at 30° to the horizontal and vertical edges vertical. In oblique drawing, the front face is drawn in true shape while receding depth lines follow the chosen oblique angle and scale. Do not confuse pictorial views with orthographic views.","revision":[]},{"title":"8. First-Angle and Third-Angle Projection","note":"These are systems for arranging orthographic views. In first-angle projection, the object is between the observer and the projection plane; the plan is placed below the front view and the right-side view appears to the left. In third-angle projection, the projection plane is between observer and object; the plan is above the front view and the right-side view appears to the right. Always check the projection symbol on the drawing.","revision":[]},{"title":"9. Sectional Views","note":"A section reveals internal details by imagining the object cut by a plane. The cut surfaces are shown with section lining (hatching), usually evenly spaced. Do not hatch empty spaces or separate parts that are not cut. A cutting-plane line indicates where the imaginary cut is made.","revision":[]},{"title":"10. Development of Surfaces","note":"Development unfolds a 3D surface into a flat pattern. Prisms and cylinders are commonly developed by laying out their side faces or circumference and height. For a cylinder, the lateral development is a rectangle whose length is the circumference πd and whose width is the cylinder height. Add allowances only when the question specifies them.","revision":[]},{"title":"11. Intersections of Solids","note":"Intersection drawings show where two solids meet or pass through one another. Find key points where edges or surface generators intersect, transfer those points between views, then join them in the correct order. Visible portions use continuous lines; hidden portions use dashed lines where appropriate.","revision":[]},{"title":"12. Auxiliary Projection","note":"Auxiliary views reveal the true shape or size of an inclined surface that appears foreshortened in the principal views. Project perpendicular to the inclined surface onto an auxiliary plane, transferring distances carefully from the related view.","revision":[]},{"title":"13. Assembly and Detail Drawing","note":"A detail drawing gives enough information to manufacture one component, including shape, dimensions, material and tolerances where required. An assembly drawing shows how components fit together and may include item numbers and a parts list. Avoid duplicating dimensions or leaving critical sizes unspecified.","revision":[]},{"title":"14. Building Drawing","note":"Building drawings can include plans, elevations, sections and site plans. A floor plan is a horizontal view showing room layout and wall positions. An elevation shows the external face from a chosen direction. Read scales, symbols, dimensions and north arrows carefully; local standards may vary.","revision":[]},{"title":"15. Electrical and Mechanical Symbols","note":"Standard symbols represent components more clearly than lengthy labels. Use the symbol legend provided and do not invent symbols. Mechanical drawings may include fasteners, bearings and joints; electrical drawings may include switches, lamps, sockets and protective devices. Symbols and conventions must be consistent throughout a drawing.","revision":[]},{"title":"16. Freehand Sketching","note":"Freehand sketches communicate an idea without relying on drawing instruments for every line. Observe proportions, main shapes, alignment and important features first. Use light guidelines, then strengthen final outlines. A sketch should be clear and correctly proportioned even if it is not perfectly scaled.","revision":[]},{"title":"17. Working Drawings and Interpretation","note":"A working drawing communicates the information needed to make or build an object. Read title block, scale, projection symbol, line types, dimensions and notes before interpreting it. Check that views agree and that units are consistent. Never infer a missing dimension unless the question gives enough information.","revision":[]},{"title":"Quick Revision Checklist","note":"• Learn the purpose of each instrument and common line types. • Practise first-angle and third-angle view arrangement. • Convert drawing measurements using the stated scale. • Distinguish orthographic, isometric and oblique drawings. • Apply section lining, development and dimensioning conventions consistently. • Use the projection symbol and any supplied legend rather than guessing.","revision":["Learn the purpose of each instrument and common line types.","Practise first-angle and third-angle view arrangement.","Convert drawing measurements using the stated scale.","Distinguish orthographic, isometric and oblique drawings.","Apply section lining, development and dimensioning conventions consistently.","Use the projection symbol and any supplied legend rather than guessing."]}],"questions":[{"q":"Which instrument is most suitable for drawing a circle of a specified radius?","o":["Compass","T-square","Set square","Scale rule"],"a":0,"explanation":"A compass draws circles and arcs with a set radius."},{"q":"A perpendicular bisector meets a line segment at","o":["30°","45°","90°","180°"],"a":2,"explanation":"A perpendicular bisector crosses the segment at a right angle."},{"q":"The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is","o":["360°","540°","720°","900°"],"a":2,"explanation":"(6−2)×180°=720°."},{"q":"The locus of points a fixed distance from one fixed point is a","o":["Straight line","Circle","Parabola only","Rectangle ## Scales and Dimensioning"],"a":1,"explanation":"All points at fixed distance r from a centre form a circle."},{"q":"A scale of 1:50 means 2 cm on the drawing represents","o":["25 cm","50 cm","100 cm","1,000 cm"],"a":2,"explanation":"2 cm×50=100 cm in reality."},{"q":"Which line is normally used to indicate an edge hidden from view?","o":["Dashed line","Thick continuous line","Freehand border","Dimension line"],"a":0,"explanation":"Dashed lines conventionally represent hidden edges."},{"q":"Dimension figures are used mainly to show","o":["The colour of a part","The required size","The weight of paper","The drawing's author only ## Projection"],"a":1,"explanation":"Dimensions communicate required lengths and sizes."},{"q":"Orthographic projection represents an object using","o":["One photograph","Views projected perpendicular to planes","Shading only","A single perspective view"],"a":1,"explanation":"Orthographic views are projected perpendicular to projection planes."},{"q":"In first-angle projection, the plan is normally placed","o":["Above the front view","Below the front view","Inside the title block","To the far right in every case"],"a":1,"explanation":"In first-angle projection, the plan is below the front view."},{"q":"In third-angle projection, the plan is normally placed","o":["Above the front view","Below the front view","Inside the front view","Under the title block"],"a":0,"explanation":"In third-angle projection, the plan is above the front view."},{"q":"An isometric drawing conventionally has its two receding axes at about","o":["15°","30°","45°","60° to the horizontal"],"a":1,"explanation":"The receding isometric axes are conventionally at 30° to the horizontal."},{"q":"In an oblique drawing, the front face is usually shown","o":["In true shape","As a circle in every case","Without edges","At random scale ## Sections and Developments"],"a":0,"explanation":"Oblique drawing normally keeps the front face in true shape."},{"q":"Section lining is used to identify","o":["Surfaces cut by the section plane","All empty spaces","Dimensions only","Hidden edges only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Hatching indicates material intersected by the cutting plane."},{"q":"The lateral development of a cylinder is a","o":["Triangle","Rectangle","Circle","Trapezium"],"a":1,"explanation":"Unrolling the cylinder's side produces a rectangle."},{"q":"The length of the rectangular lateral development of a cylinder of diameter d is","o":["d/2","2d","πd","πd² ## Interpretation and Application"],"a":2,"explanation":"The circumference is πd, which becomes the rectangle's length."},{"q":"An auxiliary view is especially useful for showing the true shape of","o":["An inclined surface","A title block","A dimension arrow","A hidden line only"],"a":0,"explanation":"An auxiliary projection can show an inclined face in true size and shape."},{"q":"A floor plan is generally a view looking","o":["Horizontally at the roof","Down onto the building layout","Up from the foundation","Only at the front elevation"],"a":1,"explanation":"A floor plan is a top-down representation of the layout."},{"q":"An assembly drawing mainly shows","o":["How components fit together","Only one isolated material sample","A list of colours","A map of the site only"],"a":0,"explanation":"An assembly drawing explains the relationship between assembled parts."},{"q":"A parts list may include component names and","o":["Item numbers and quantities","Weather reports","Exam scores","Unrelated paragraphs"],"a":0,"explanation":"Parts lists commonly identify items and quantities."},{"q":"Before interpreting a technical drawing, you should check the","o":["Title block, scale and projection symbol","Paper colour only","Artist's favourite colour","Number of eraser marks"],"a":0,"explanation":"These drawing details establish scale, orientation and projection conventions."}]},"Government":{"lessons":[{"title":"Meaning and Scope of Government","note":"Government is the institution and process through which a society makes and enforces collective decisions. It maintains order, provides public services, protects rights and manages relations with other states. Political science studies power, authority, legitimacy, institutions and political behaviour. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Meaning and Scope of Government.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Constitution","note":"A constitution is the set of fundamental rules by which a state is governed. It may be written or unwritten, rigid or flexible, federal or unitary in character. It allocates powers, establishes institutions and may protect citizens’ rights. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Constitution.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Arms of Government","note":"The legislature makes laws and scrutinises government; the executive implements laws and policies; the judiciary interprets laws and settles disputes. Separation of powers and checks and balances are intended to limit excessive concentration of authority. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Arms of Government.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Systems of Government","note":"In a presidential system, the president is separately elected and serves as head of state and government. In a parliamentary system, the executive usually depends on the confidence of the legislature. Federal systems divide constitutional powers between central and regional governments. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Systems of Government.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Political Parties and Pressure Groups","note":"Political parties seek political power through elections and organise candidates, policies and government. Pressure groups try to influence decisions without necessarily seeking to govern. They may use lobbying, campaigns, petitions and public education. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Political Parties and Pressure Groups.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Elections and Electoral Systems","note":"Elections allow eligible voters to choose representatives or decide public questions. Credible elections require impartial administration, clear rules, voter education, secrecy of the ballot and transparent counting. Electoral systems include simple majority and proportional representation. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Elections and Electoral Systems.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Citizenship and Rights","note":"Citizenship is legal membership of a state, carrying rights and duties. Rights may include fair hearing, expression and association, subject to lawful limits. Duties commonly include obeying the law, respecting others and contributing to public life. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Citizenship and Rights.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Public Administration and Local Government","note":"Public administration implements public policy through ministries, departments and agencies. Local government brings some public decisions closer to communities and may provide basic local services. Accountability, merit, transparency and responsible use of public funds are essential. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Public Administration and Local Government.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"Which arm of government is primarily responsible for making laws?","o":["Executive","Legislature","Judiciary","Civil service"],"a":1,"explanation":"The legislature debates and enacts laws; the executive implements them and the judiciary interprets them.","topic":"Meaning and Scope of Government"},{"q":"A constitution mainly provides","o":["rules for governing the state","a list of private businesses","weather forecasts","only election results"],"a":0,"explanation":"A constitution establishes the basic rules, institutions and powers of the state.","topic":"Constitution"},{"q":"In a presidential system, the president is generally","o":["appointed by the judiciary","head of state and government","the leader of the legislature only","a permanent civil servant"],"a":1,"explanation":"The president commonly combines the roles of head of state and head of government.","topic":"Arms of Government"},{"q":"A pressure group differs from a political party because a pressure group usually seeks to","o":["influence policy without directly forming government","abolish all public debate","appoint every judge","replace elections with lotteries"],"a":0,"explanation":"Pressure groups influence decisions but do not normally seek to win and exercise governmental power themselves.","topic":"Systems of Government"},{"q":"The secret ballot is intended mainly to protect","o":["voter privacy and freedom of choice","the secrecy of public budgets","the identity of candidates after results","the counting officials from all scrutiny"],"a":0,"explanation":"Ballot secrecy reduces intimidation and allows voters to choose freely.","topic":"Political Parties and Pressure Groups"},{"q":"The principle that public power should be divided among branches is called","o":["hereditary rule","separation of powers","one-party rule","military command"],"a":1,"explanation":"Separation of powers distributes functions among the legislature, executive and judiciary.","topic":"Elections and Electoral Systems"}]},"Economics":{"lessons":[{"title":"Meaning and Basic Concepts","note":"Economics studies how people and societies allocate scarce resources among competing wants. Scarcity means resources are limited relative to wants; choice is therefore necessary. Opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Meaning and Basic Concepts.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Demand","note":"Demand is the quantity consumers are willing and able to buy at different prices over a period. Other things equal, quantity demanded usually rises when price falls. Income, tastes, population, expectations and prices of related goods can shift demand. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Demand.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Supply","note":"Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to offer at different prices. Other things equal, a higher price tends to encourage a larger quantity supplied. Production costs, technology, taxes, subsidies and weather can shift supply. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Supply.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Price Determination","note":"Market equilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. A price above equilibrium may create a surplus; a price below equilibrium may create a shortage. Changes in demand or supply can alter equilibrium price and quantity. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Price Determination.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Production and Factors of Production","note":"Production creates goods and services or adds utility. The main factors are land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship, each receiving a corresponding reward in simplified models: rent, wages, interest and profit. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Production and Factors of Production.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Market Structures","note":"Perfect competition has many buyers and sellers and relatively easy entry; monopoly has a single dominant supplier with high barriers to entry. Monopolistic competition features differentiated products, while oligopoly has a few interdependent large firms. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Market Structures.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Money, Banking and Inflation","note":"Money serves as a medium of exchange, unit of account and store of value. Commercial banks accept deposits and provide payment and lending services. Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level, reducing the purchasing power of money. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Money, Banking and Inflation.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"National Income and Public Finance","note":"National income measures economic activity using concepts such as GDP. Fiscal policy uses taxation and public spending to influence the economy. A budget sets out expected public revenue and expenditure; deficits occur when expenditure exceeds revenue. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in National Income and Public Finance.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"The next best alternative forgone when a choice is made is","o":["fixed cost","opportunity cost","profit margin","national income"],"a":1,"explanation":"Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative sacrificed.","topic":"Meaning and Basic Concepts"},{"q":"According to the usual law of demand, when price falls, quantity demanded tends to","o":["fall","rise","become zero","remain fixed in every case"],"a":1,"explanation":"Other things equal, lower prices generally increase quantity demanded.","topic":"Demand"},{"q":"Which is a factor of production?","o":["Inflation","Land","Tax rate","Budget deficit"],"a":1,"explanation":"Land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship are the conventional factors of production.","topic":"Supply"},{"q":"Market equilibrium occurs where","o":["supply is always zero","quantity demanded equals quantity supplied","government spending equals exports","every firm earns the same profit"],"a":1,"explanation":"Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which planned demand equals planned supply.","topic":"Price Determination"},{"q":"A sustained increase in the general price level is called","o":["deflation","inflation","devaluation only","depression"],"a":1,"explanation":"Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level.","topic":"Production and Factors of Production"},{"q":"The main role of a central bank commonly includes","o":["conducting monetary policy","selling every household’s goods","marking school examinations","running all private farms"],"a":0,"explanation":"Central banks conduct monetary policy and commonly oversee currency and aspects of the banking system.","topic":"Market Structures"}]},"Literature in English":{"lessons":[{"title":"Literature and Genres","note":"Literature uses language imaginatively to explore human experience. The major genres are prose, poetry and drama. Analyse what the text says, how it is constructed and how its choices create meaning; support interpretations with evidence. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Literature and Genres.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Prose: Plot and Structure","note":"Plot is the arrangement of events in a narrative. Exposition introduces the setting and characters; conflict creates tension; climax is the turning point; resolution shows how the main conflict ends. Flashback and foreshadowing alter how readers receive information. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Prose: Plot and Structure.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Characterisation","note":"Characterisation is how a writer presents a character through speech, action, thoughts, appearance and other characters’ reactions. A protagonist is central to the action; an antagonist opposes the protagonist’s aims. Characters may be dynamic or static. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Characterisation.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Setting and Theme","note":"Setting includes time, place and social environment. Theme is a central idea explored by the text, such as power, betrayal, identity or justice. A theme is not merely a topic; explain what the text suggests about that topic. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Setting and Theme.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Poetry: Form and Sound","note":"Study the speaker, situation, stanza, rhythm, rhyme, sound patterns and imagery. The speaker is the voice of the poem and is not automatically the poet. Form and sound can reinforce mood, contrast or emphasis. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Poetry: Form and Sound.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Figures of Speech","note":"A simile compares using ‘like’ or ‘as’; a metaphor describes one thing in terms of another; personification gives human qualities to non-human things. Irony involves a contrast between expectation and reality, while hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Figures of Speech.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Drama and Dramatic Techniques","note":"Drama is written for performance. Dialogue reveals relationships and conflict; stage directions guide action and delivery. Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows something a character does not. Soliloquies and asides can reveal private thoughts. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Drama and Dramatic Techniques.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Literary Criticism and Context","note":"Interpret a text in its historical, cultural and literary context without assuming context alone determines meaning. Distinguish a narrator from an author, a theme from a plot, and a character’s opinion from the text’s overall position. Use precise textual evidence. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Literary Criticism and Context.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"A comparison that explicitly uses ‘like’ or ‘as’ is a","o":["metaphor","simile","paradox","pun"],"a":1,"explanation":"A simile makes a comparison using words such as ‘like’ or ‘as’.","topic":"Literature and Genres"},{"q":"The turning point of greatest tension in a plot is usually the","o":["exposition","climax","setting","epilogue"],"a":1,"explanation":"The climax is the major turning point or peak of dramatic tension.","topic":"Prose: Plot and Structure"},{"q":"The voice speaking in a poem is called the","o":["publisher","speaker","stage manager","critic"],"a":1,"explanation":"The speaker is the voice of the poem and should not automatically be identified with the poet.","topic":"Characterisation"},{"q":"A character who changes significantly during a story is","o":["static","dynamic","incidental in every case","anonymous"],"a":1,"explanation":"A dynamic character undergoes meaningful change during the narrative.","topic":"Setting and Theme"},{"q":"When an audience knows a fact that a character does not, the technique is","o":["dramatic irony","alliteration","onomatopoeia","rhyme"],"a":0,"explanation":"Dramatic irony creates a gap between audience knowledge and a character’s knowledge.","topic":"Poetry: Form and Sound"},{"q":"A central idea explored by a literary work is its","o":["theme","setting","stage direction","rhyme scheme only"],"a":0,"explanation":"A theme is an idea the work develops, often through events, characters and imagery.","topic":"Figures of Speech"}]},"Geography":{"lessons":[{"title":"Meaning and Scope of Geography","note":"Geography studies places, environments and relationships between people and the physical world. Physical geography examines natural systems; human geography studies populations, settlements, economic activity and spatial patterns. Maps and fieldwork help gather and interpret evidence. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Meaning and Scope of Geography.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"The Earth and Its Movements","note":"Earth rotates on its axis, producing day and night, and revolves around the Sun. Earth’s axial tilt combined with revolution causes seasonal changes. Latitude measures angular distance north or south of the Equator; longitude measures east or west of the Prime Meridian. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in The Earth and Its Movements.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Weather and Climate","note":"Weather describes atmospheric conditions over a short period; climate summarises patterns over many years. Temperature, rainfall, humidity, pressure and wind are key elements. Latitude, altitude, distance from the sea, ocean currents and relief influence climate. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Weather and Climate.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Rocks and Landforms","note":"Igneous rocks form from cooled magma or lava; sedimentary rocks form from deposited material; metamorphic rocks change under heat and pressure. Weathering breaks rock down in place, erosion transports material, and deposition lays it down. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Rocks and Landforms.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Rivers and Drainage","note":"A drainage basin is the area drained by a river and its tributaries. Upper courses commonly have steep gradients and vertical erosion; lower courses often have wider valleys, meanders and floodplains. Discharge depends on factors including rainfall, basin size and surface conditions. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Rivers and Drainage.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Population and Settlement","note":"Population distribution describes where people live; density is population divided by land area. Birth rate, death rate and migration affect population change. Settlements may be rural or urban, and their patterns are influenced by water, relief, transport, jobs and services. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Population and Settlement.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Economic Activities and Resources","note":"Primary activities extract or harvest resources; secondary activities process materials; tertiary activities provide services. Resource use depends on accessibility, technology, demand and sustainability. Economic activities often cluster near transport routes, markets, labour and raw materials. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Economic Activities and Resources.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Maps, Scale and Fieldwork","note":"A map scale relates map distance to ground distance. At a scale of 1:50,000, 1 cm on the map represents 50,000 cm (500 m) on the ground. Contour lines join points of equal elevation. Fieldwork should use a clear question, reliable observations and careful recording. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Maps, Scale and Fieldwork.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"The imaginary line at 0° latitude is the","o":["Prime Meridian","Equator","Tropic of Capricorn","Arctic Circle"],"a":1,"explanation":"The Equator is the reference line for 0° latitude.","topic":"Meaning and Scope of Geography"},{"q":"The long-term average pattern of atmospheric conditions is","o":["weather","climate","humidity only","a daily forecast"],"a":1,"explanation":"Climate describes atmospheric patterns over a long period, unlike day-to-day weather.","topic":"The Earth and Its Movements"},{"q":"Which rock type forms when magma cools and solidifies?","o":["Sedimentary","Igneous","Metamorphic","Organic soil"],"a":1,"explanation":"Igneous rocks form from cooled magma or lava.","topic":"Weather and Climate"},{"q":"At a map scale of 1:50,000, 1 cm represents","o":["50 m","500 m","5 km","50 km"],"a":1,"explanation":"50,000 cm equals 500 m.","topic":"Rocks and Landforms"},{"q":"Population density is calculated as","o":["land area divided by rainfall","population divided by land area","births minus exports","imports divided by population"],"a":1,"explanation":"Population density is the number of people per unit of land area.","topic":"Rivers and Drainage"},{"q":"The breaking down of rock in place is called","o":["weathering","transportation","deposition","condensation"],"a":0,"explanation":"Weathering breaks down rock in place; erosion transports material away.","topic":"Population and Settlement"}]},"Commerce":{"lessons":[{"title":"Meaning and Scope of Commerce","note":"Commerce concerns the distribution and exchange of goods and services. Trade is buying and selling; aids to trade include transport, communication, banking, insurance, warehousing and advertising. These services help goods move from producers to consumers. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Meaning and Scope of Commerce.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Trade and Channels of Distribution","note":"Home trade occurs within a country; foreign trade occurs between countries. Wholesalers usually buy in bulk and sell to retailers, while retailers sell to final consumers. Some producers sell directly to consumers, shortening the distribution channel. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Trade and Channels of Distribution.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Retail and Wholesale Trade","note":"Retailers provide convenient quantities, locations and product information to consumers. Wholesalers can provide bulk breaking, storage, transport and market information to retailers. Digital commerce changes how buyers and sellers discover, order and pay for goods. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Retail and Wholesale Trade.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Transport and Communication","note":"Transport moves people and goods by road, rail, water, air or pipeline. Choice depends on distance, cost, speed, cargo type and infrastructure. Communication transfers information through channels such as phone, email and online platforms. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Transport and Communication.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Banking and Payment Systems","note":"Banks provide deposit, payment, lending and other financial services. Common payment methods include cash, transfers, cards and electronic payment systems. Users should protect PINs and passwords, verify recipients and retain transaction records. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Banking and Payment Systems.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Insurance and Risk","note":"Insurance transfers specified financial risks from a policyholder to an insurer in return for a premium, subject to policy terms. Insurable interest, utmost good faith and indemnity are key principles in many forms of general insurance; life insurance operates under distinct terms. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Insurance and Risk.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Advertising and Sales Promotion","note":"Advertising is paid communication intended to inform or persuade a target audience. Sales promotion uses short-term incentives such as discounts or samples. Effective marketing identifies the audience, presents accurate claims and chooses suitable channels. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Advertising and Sales Promotion.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"International Trade and Documents","note":"Imports are goods and services bought from abroad; exports are sold abroad. Trade documents can include invoices, bills of lading, certificates of origin and customs declarations. Exchange rates and trade policies affect international transactions. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in International Trade and Documents.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"Which is an aid to trade?","o":["Warehousing","Photosynthesis","Weathering","Pollination"],"a":0,"explanation":"Warehousing stores goods until they are needed, supporting distribution.","topic":"Meaning and Scope of Commerce"},{"q":"A retailer normally sells goods to","o":["the final consumer","only the manufacturer","the central bank","foreign customs officers"],"a":0,"explanation":"Retailers generally sell goods in consumer quantities to final users.","topic":"Trade and Channels of Distribution"},{"q":"Buying goods from another country is called","o":["exporting","importing","bartering only","retailing"],"a":1,"explanation":"Imports are goods or services purchased from abroad.","topic":"Retail and Wholesale Trade"},{"q":"The amount paid for an insurance policy is the","o":["premium","dividend","tariff","commission in every case"],"a":0,"explanation":"The premium is the payment made for the insurance cover under the policy.","topic":"Transport and Communication"},{"q":"Which transport mode is commonly suited to bulky international cargo over long distances at low unit cost?","o":["Sea transport","Motorcycle delivery","Walking","Local bicycle"],"a":0,"explanation":"Sea freight is often economical for bulky international cargo, though slower than air freight.","topic":"Banking and Payment Systems"},{"q":"A wholesaler mainly helps by","o":["buying in bulk and supplying retailers","selling only one item to each household","issuing national currency","setting examination questions"],"a":0,"explanation":"Wholesalers purchase in bulk and redistribute goods to retailers.","topic":"Insurance and Risk"}]},"Principles of Accounts":{"lessons":[{"title":"Purpose of Accounting","note":"Accounting identifies, records, classifies and summarises financial transactions so users can make decisions. Bookkeeping is the systematic recording of transactions; accounting also interprets and communicates financial information. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Purpose of Accounting.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Accounting Equation","note":"The basic accounting equation is Assets = Capital + Liabilities. Assets are resources controlled by a business, liabilities are obligations to outsiders, and capital represents the owner's residual interest. Every transaction must preserve the equation. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Accounting Equation.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Double-Entry Bookkeeping","note":"Each transaction affects at least two accounts, with total debits equal to total credits. Debit and credit mean the left and right sides of an account; they do not automatically mean increase and decrease for every account type. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Double-Entry Bookkeeping.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Source Documents and Books of Original Entry","note":"Invoices, receipts, credit notes, debit notes and payment vouchers provide evidence of transactions. Transactions are first recorded in suitable books of original entry, such as sales journals, purchases journals, cash books or general journals. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Source Documents and Books of Original Entry.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Ledger and Trial Balance","note":"Ledger accounts group transactions by account. Balancing an account determines its net balance. A trial balance lists ledger balances to test arithmetic equality of debits and credits, but it does not reveal every error. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Ledger and Trial Balance.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Cash Book and Bank Reconciliation","note":"A cash book records cash and bank transactions. A bank reconciliation explains differences between the cash book and bank statement, such as unpresented cheques, deposits not yet credited and bank charges not yet recorded. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Cash Book and Bank Reconciliation.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Trading, Profit and Loss Accounts","note":"A trading account calculates gross profit: sales less cost of goods sold. A profit and loss account accounts for expenses and other income to calculate net profit or loss. Distinguish gross profit from net profit. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Trading, Profit and Loss Accounts.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Balance Sheet and Depreciation","note":"A statement of financial position reports assets, liabilities and capital at a date. Depreciation allocates the depreciable amount of a non-current asset over its useful life; it is not simply a cash fund set aside for replacement. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Balance Sheet and Depreciation.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"The basic accounting equation is","o":["Assets = Capital + Liabilities","Assets = Sales − Purchases","Capital = Expenses + Drawings","Liabilities = Assets + Revenue"],"a":0,"explanation":"The accounting equation states that assets are financed by capital and liabilities.","topic":"Purpose of Accounting"},{"q":"The left-hand side of a ledger account is the","o":["credit side","debit side","balance side only","closing side"],"a":1,"explanation":"The debit side is the left side of an account; the credit side is the right.","topic":"Accounting Equation"},{"q":"The main purpose of a trial balance is to check","o":["arithmetic equality of debit and credit balances","that every transaction is fraud-free","the market price of stock","the owner's age"],"a":0,"explanation":"A trial balance checks whether total debit balances equal total credit balances, but some errors may remain.","topic":"Double-Entry Bookkeeping"},{"q":"A source document for a credit sale commonly includes a","o":["sales invoice","weather map","birth certificate","contour map"],"a":0,"explanation":"A sales invoice records details of goods sold on credit.","topic":"Source Documents and Books of Original Entry"},{"q":"Gross profit is generally calculated as","o":["sales minus cost of goods sold","assets minus liabilities","capital plus drawings","cash receipts minus bank charges only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Gross profit is sales revenue less the cost of goods sold.","topic":"Ledger and Trial Balance"},{"q":"Depreciation is best described as","o":["allocation of an asset's depreciable amount over its useful life","a cash deposit paid by every customer","an increase in the asset's market price","a bank reconciliation item in all cases"],"a":0,"explanation":"Depreciation systematically allocates an asset's depreciable amount over its useful life.","topic":"Cash Book and Bank Reconciliation"}]},"History":{"lessons":[{"title":"Meaning and Sources of History","note":"History studies and interprets past human events using evidence. Primary sources originate from the period or participants being studied; secondary sources interpret or analyse evidence later. Historians compare sources, context, authorship, purpose and reliability. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Meaning and Sources of History.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Pre-colonial African Societies","note":"African societies developed diverse political and economic systems, including kingdoms, empires, city-states and decentralised communities. Their organisation varied by region and period; avoid treating Africa as one uniform society. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Pre-colonial African Societies.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Trade and State Formation","note":"Trade routes supported the exchange of goods, ideas, technology and religion. Control of trade, taxation, agriculture, military organisation and leadership could contribute to state formation, while conflict and environmental change could weaken states. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Trade and State Formation.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"European Contact and the Atlantic Slave Trade","note":"European contact varied across time and region. The Atlantic slave trade forcibly transported millions of Africans and reshaped communities and economies across Africa, Europe and the Americas. Study its causes, methods, consequences and resistance using reliable historical evidence. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in European Contact and the Atlantic Slave Trade.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Colonial Rule and Resistance","note":"Colonial rule imposed foreign political and economic authority through different systems and policies. African responses included armed resistance, diplomacy, protest, adaptation and political organisation. Case studies should be examined in their local context. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Colonial Rule and Resistance.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Nationalism and Independence","note":"Nationalist movements sought self-government through political parties, newspapers, unions, protests, negotiations and sometimes armed struggle. Economic change, education, international developments and post-war politics influenced decolonisation. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Nationalism and Independence.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Post-independence Challenges","note":"New states faced tasks such as nation-building, economic development, constitutional government and managing diversity. Outcomes differed by country; explanations should consider institutions, leadership, external pressures and local conditions. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Post-independence Challenges.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Historical Evidence and Chronology","note":"Chronology arranges events in time. Cause explains why an event occurred; consequence explains what followed. Strong historical answers distinguish evidence from opinion and consider multiple causes rather than assuming one event had only one explanation. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Historical Evidence and Chronology.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"A source created by a participant during the period being studied is usually a","o":["primary source","secondary source","fictional source by definition","modern textbook only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Primary sources originate from the period or participants being studied.","topic":"Meaning and Sources of History"},{"q":"Chronology is the study or arrangement of","o":["events in time order","soil layers only","market prices","political slogans"],"a":0,"explanation":"Chronology places events in their temporal sequence.","topic":"Pre-colonial African Societies"},{"q":"Which is an example of a secondary historical source?","o":["A later historian's analysis of letters and records","A letter written by a participant at the time","An original treaty document","A contemporary diary"],"a":0,"explanation":"A later historian's analysis interprets primary evidence and is a secondary source.","topic":"Trade and State Formation"},{"q":"A major aim of African nationalist movements was","o":["self-government and independence","permanent foreign rule","ending all education","abolishing historical records"],"a":0,"explanation":"Nationalist movements generally sought self-rule and political independence.","topic":"European Contact and the Atlantic Slave Trade"},{"q":"When evaluating a historical source, a historian should consider its","o":["author, context and purpose","paper size only","colour only","price alone"],"a":0,"explanation":"Authorship, context, purpose and corroboration help assess reliability and meaning.","topic":"Colonial Rule and Resistance"},{"q":"A consequence of an event is","o":["an outcome that followed it","the only possible cause","the name of the author","always unrelated to the event"],"a":0,"explanation":"Consequences are effects or outcomes that follow an event.","topic":"Nationalism and Independence"}]},"Christian Religious Studies":{"lessons":[{"title":"The Bible and Its Structure","note":"The Christian Bible contains the Old Testament and New Testament. Biblical books include different genres such as narrative, law, poetry, prophecy, gospel, letters and apocalyptic writing. Interpret passages with attention to genre, context and the passage's place in the wider text. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in The Bible and Its Structure.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Creation and Human Responsibility","note":"Genesis presents accounts of creation and humanity's place within it. In Christian teaching, creation is commonly linked with human responsibility, stewardship and accountability. Read the passage carefully and distinguish its stated claims from later interpretation. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Creation and Human Responsibility.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Covenant and the Patriarchs","note":"Biblical covenants describe relationships and commitments between God and people. Accounts of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob include promises, family conflict, journeys and tests of faith. Identify the promise or command, the response and the consequence in each narrative. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Covenant and the Patriarchs.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Moses, Exodus and the Law","note":"The Exodus narrative describes Israel's departure from Egypt and its journey toward the promised land. Moses is central to the account, and the giving of the law establishes religious and communal responsibilities. Questions may test events, leadership and lessons drawn from the text. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Moses, Exodus and the Law.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Prophets and Social Justice","note":"Biblical prophets communicated messages calling people to faithfulness and often condemning injustice, exploitation and empty ritual. Interpret a prophetic passage by identifying its audience, the behaviour criticised and the change demanded. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Prophets and Social Justice.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"The Life and Teaching of Jesus","note":"The Gospels recount Jesus' ministry, teachings, miracles, death and resurrection. Parables use stories to communicate lessons; the Sermon on the Mount includes teachings about character, prayer, reconciliation and ethical conduct. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in The Life and Teaching of Jesus.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"The Early Church","note":"Acts describes the growth of the early Christian community, the work of the apostles, Pentecost, preaching and the spread of the message beyond Jerusalem. The letters address belief, worship, community conduct and disputes. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in The Early Church.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Christian Ethics and Daily Life","note":"Christian ethics commonly emphasises love of neighbour, honesty, forgiveness, justice, service and care for vulnerable people. Explain a teaching in its biblical context and show how it applies without ignoring the specific question. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Christian Ethics and Daily Life.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"The first book of the Christian Bible is","o":["Genesis","Psalms","Matthew","Acts"],"a":0,"explanation":"Genesis opens the Bible and contains creation narratives and accounts of the patriarchs.","topic":"The Bible and Its Structure"},{"q":"The Exodus narrative is centrally associated with the leadership of","o":["Moses","Solomon","Nehemiah","Luke"],"a":0,"explanation":"Moses is the central leader in the biblical account of Israel's departure from Egypt.","topic":"Creation and Human Responsibility"},{"q":"A parable is best described as","o":["a story used to communicate a lesson","a list of census figures only","a musical instrument","a Roman tax"],"a":0,"explanation":"Parables use familiar stories or comparisons to communicate teaching.","topic":"Covenant and the Patriarchs"},{"q":"The Acts of the Apostles mainly describes","o":["the early Christian community and its mission","the building of Noah's ark","the reign of Pharaoh in Exodus","the fall of Jericho only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Acts recounts the work of the apostles and the spread of the early Christian movement.","topic":"Moses, Exodus and the Law"},{"q":"Biblical prophets frequently challenged","o":["injustice and unfaithfulness","all forms of compassion","the existence of language","the use of chronology"],"a":0,"explanation":"Many prophetic messages confront injustice, exploitation and unfaithfulness.","topic":"Prophets and Social Justice"},{"q":"The New Testament includes the four","o":["Gospels","books of the Pentateuch","major prophets only","historical records of every empire"],"a":0,"explanation":"The four canonical Gospels are Matthew, Mark, Luke and John.","topic":"The Life and Teaching of Jesus"}]},"Islamic Religious Studies":{"lessons":[{"title":"The Qur'an and Revelation","note":"The Qur'an is the central scripture of Islam, understood by Muslims as revelation to Prophet Muhammad. It is organised into surahs and ayahs. Study questions may address its revelation, preservation, themes and guidance, using the prescribed text and syllabus. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in The Qur'an and Revelation.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Hadith and Sunnah","note":"Hadith reports describe sayings, actions or approvals attributed to Prophet Muhammad; Sunnah refers to his normative example. Hadith studies examine transmission and reliability. Distinguish the Qur'an from hadith and follow the classification required by the syllabus. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Hadith and Sunnah.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"The Life of Prophet Muhammad","note":"Study the Prophet's life in chronological context, including early life, the first revelation, migration to Madinah, community formation and key events. Explain causes and consequences rather than memorising isolated dates alone. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in The Life of Prophet Muhammad.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"The Five Pillars of Islam","note":"The five pillars are shahadah, salah, zakah, sawm during Ramadan and hajj for those able to perform it. They express faith, worship, charity, self-discipline and communal responsibility. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in The Five Pillars of Islam.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Faith and Worship","note":"Islamic belief includes belief in Allah, angels, revealed books, messengers, the Last Day and divine decree. Worship practices have prescribed conditions and purposes; study the relevant details from reliable Islamic teaching and the syllabus. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Faith and Worship.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Islamic Ethics and Social Relations","note":"Islamic ethical teaching includes honesty, justice, mercy, fulfilling trusts, caring for neighbours and avoiding oppression. Apply the teaching to the question's context and distinguish a general principle from a specific legal ruling. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Islamic Ethics and Social Relations.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Family, Society and Community","note":"Islamic teachings address marriage, family responsibilities, inheritance, social welfare and community relations. Exact rules can depend on the issue and the prescribed syllabus; learn definitions and conditions carefully rather than relying on assumptions. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Family, Society and Community.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]},{"title":"Islamic History and the Caliphate","note":"Early Islamic history includes the community in Madinah and the period of the Rightly Guided Caliphs. Study leadership, consultation, administration, expansion and challenges in their historical context, using the course's prescribed sources. Revision focus: Define the key terms, explain the main principle in your own words, and apply it to an example or a JAMB-style question.","revision":["Define the key terms in Islamic History and the Caliphate.","Explain the main principle and support it with an example.","Practise a question and check your reasoning."]}],"questions":[{"q":"The central scripture of Islam is the","o":["Qur'an","Sunnah only","Sira only","Ijma"],"a":0,"explanation":"The Qur'an is the central scripture of Islam.","topic":"The Qur'an and Revelation"},{"q":"Salah refers to","o":["the prescribed ritual prayer","the annual pilgrimage only","charitable tax only","fasting during Ramadan"],"a":0,"explanation":"Salah is the prescribed ritual prayer performed at appointed times.","topic":"Hadith and Sunnah"},{"q":"Zakah is primarily associated with","o":["obligatory almsgiving","the call to prayer","the migration to Madinah","the declaration of faith only"],"a":0,"explanation":"Zakah is obligatory almsgiving under defined conditions.","topic":"The Life of Prophet Muhammad"},{"q":"The migration of Prophet Muhammad from Makkah to Madinah is known as","o":["Hijrah","Hajj","Sawm","Tawaf"],"a":0,"explanation":"Hijrah refers to the migration from Makkah to Madinah.","topic":"The Five Pillars of Islam"},{"q":"Hadith reports concern sayings, actions or approvals attributed to","o":["Prophet Muhammad","every later ruler without distinction","the Roman Senate","the authors of all modern textbooks"],"a":0,"explanation":"Hadith reports describe sayings, actions or approvals attributed to the Prophet.","topic":"Faith and Worship"},{"q":"Fasting during Ramadan is known as","o":["sawm","zakah","salah","shahadah"],"a":0,"explanation":"Sawm is fasting, particularly the prescribed fast during Ramadan.","topic":"Islamic Ethics and Social Relations"}]}};
